INSERT INTO runs VALUES('792e01e8-8c81-49c3-9a9f-3461398f6ef5','2026-08-10T01:07:06.404637+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium_v2','claude-sonnet-4-6',3,'Which British first-generation jet-powered medium bomber was used in the South West Pacific theatre of World War II?','There was no British first-generation jet-powered medium bomber used in the South West Pacific theatre of World War II. Britain''s first jet medium bomber, the English Electric Canberra, did not enter service until 1951, well after WWII ended. The only British jet aircraft to see operational service during WWII was the Gloster Meteor, a fighter used exclusively in Europe. The principal British-designed medium bomber operated in the South West Pacific theatre was the de Havilland Mosquito, which was piston-powered (twin Rolls-Royce Merlin engines), not jet-powered. The RAAF flew Mosquito FB Mk VI and PR variants in that theatre from 1944–1945, conducting bombing and reconnaissance missions against Japanese targets in Borneo and the Dutch East Indies. The premise of the question contains a factual error — no British jet bomber existed during WWII, let alone served in the Pacific.',1,3649,1777,1,30.50600000000000022,'hotpotqa');
