ments\n \n \n(1,828) \n \n(2,152) \n \n(1,410) \nProceeds from maturities and sale of marketable securities and investments\n \n \n2,497 \n \n1,354 \n \n1,247 \nProceeds from sale of businesses, net of cash sold\n \n \n846 \n \n1,065 \n \n142 \nOther net\n \n \n 9 \n \n(6) \n \n(4) \nNet cash provided by (used in) investing activities\n \n \n222 \n \n(3,086) \n \n(1,403) \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nCash Flows from Financing Activities\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nChange in short-term debt net\n \n \n(284) \n \n578 \n \n(797) \nRepayment of debt (maturities greater than 90 days)\n \n \n(1,034) \n \n(962) \n \n(992) \nProceeds from debt  [...evidence truncated]','\n',char(10)),'3M''s FY2018 capital expenditure, reported as ''Purchases of property, plant and equipment (PP&E)'' under Cash Flows from Investing Activities, was $1,577 million.',1,8175,1069,1,15.74099999999999966,'financebench');
INSERT INTO runs VALUES('1b2ea75b-7bd9-4de7-aafa-ac01ae7b2b18','2026-08-26T05:17:58.906935+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium','claude-sonnet-5',3,replace('Question: Assume that you are a public equities analyst. Answer the following question by primarily using information that is shown in the balance sheet: what is the year end FY2018 net PPNE for 3M? Answer in USD billions.\n\nContext from financial filing:\nTable of Contents \n3M Company and Subsidiaries\nConsolidated Balance Shee t\nAt December 31\n \n \n \nDecember 31,\n \nDecember 31,\n \n(Dollars in millions, except per share amount)\n \n2018\n \n2017\n \nAssets\n \n \n \n \n \nCurrent assets\n \n \n \n \n \nCash and cash equivalents\n \n$\n2,853 \n$\n3,053 \nMarketable securities current\n \n \n380 \n \n1,076 \nAccounts receivable net of allowances of $95 and $103\n \n \n5,020 \n \n4,911 \nInventories\n \n \n \n \n \nFinished goods\n \n \n2,120 \n \n1,915 \nWork in process\n \n \n1,292 \n \n1,218 \nRaw materials and supplies\n \n \n954 \n \n901 \nTotal inventories\n \n \n4,366 \n \n4,034 \nPrepaids\n \n \n741 \n \n937 \nOther current assets\n \n \n349 \n \n266 \nTotal current assets\n \n \n13,709 \n \n14,277 \nProperty, plant and equipment\n \n \n24,873 \n \n24,914 \nLess: Accumulated depreciation\n \n \n(16,135) \n \n(16,048) \nProperty, plant and equipment net\n \n \n8,738 \n \n8,866 \nGoodwill\n \n \n10,051 \n \n10,513 \nIntangible assets net\n \n \n2,657 \n \n2,936 \nOther assets\n \n \n1,345 \n \n1,395 \nTotal assets\n \n$\n36,500 \n$\n37,987 \nLiabilities\n \n \n \n \n \nCurrent liabilities\n \n \n \n \n \nShort-term borrowings and current portion of long-term debt\n \n$\n1,211 \n$\n1,853 \nAccounts payable\n \n \n2,266 \n \n1,945 \nAccrued payroll\n \n \n749 \n \n870 \nAccrued income taxes\n \n \n243 \n \n310 \nOther current liabilities\n \n \n2,775 \n \n2,709 \nTotal current liabilities\n \n \n7,244 \n \n7,687 \n \n \n \n \n \n \nLong-term debt\n \n \n13,411 \n \n12,096 \nPension and postretirement benefits\n \n \n2,987 \n \n3,620 \nOther liabilities\n \n \n3,010 \n \n2,962 \nTotal liabilities\n \n$\n26,652 \n$\n26,365 \nCommitments and contingencies (Note 16)\n \n \n \n \n \nEquity\n \n \n \n \n \n3M Company shareholders equity:\n \n \n \n \n \nCommon stock par value, $.01 par value\n \n$\n 9 \n$\n 9 \nShares outstanding - 2018: 576,575,168\n \n \n \n \n \nShares outstanding - 2017: 594,884,237\n \n \n \n \n \nAdditional paid-in capital\n \n \n5,643 \n \n5,352 \nRetained earnings\n \n \n40,636 \n \n39,115 \nTreasury stock\n \n \n(29,626) \n \n(25,887) \nAccumulated other comprehensive income (loss)\n \n \n(6,866) \n \n(7,026) \nTotal 3M Company shareholders equity\n \n \n9,796  [...evidence truncated]','\n',char(10)),'3M''s net property, plant and equipment (PP&E) at fiscal year end 2018 (December 31, 2018) was $8,738 million, or approximately $8.7 billion, calculated as gross PP&E of $24,873 million less accumulated depreciation of $16,135 million.',1,8368,1089,1,14.71799999999999998,'financebench');
INSERT INTO runs VALUES('fcd60942-f3e5-4491-80e9-91f6e3b36336','2026-08-26T05:18:22.511170+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium','claude-sonnet-5',3,replace('Question: Is 3M a capital-intensive business based on FY2022 data?\n\nContext from financial filing:\n3M Company and Subsidiaries\nConsolidated Statement of Income\nYears ended December 31\n(Millions, except per share amounts)\n2022\n2021\n2020\nNet sales\n$\n34,229 $\n35,355 $\n32,184','\n',char(10)),'Yes, 3M is capital-intensive. FY2022 filing data shows only net sales ($34,229M, down 3.2% from 2021) without CapEx/PP&E figures, but 3M''s diversified manufacturing model (adhesives, electronics, healthcare) historically requires $1.5-1.8B annual CapEx (~4-5% of revenue), typical of capital-intensive industries.',1,7410,1866,3,23.59900000000000019,'financebench');
INSERT INTO runs VALUES('89d5a568-f69f-4a28-8758-7f3f59e7cd17','2026-08-26T05:18:44.179008+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium','claude-sonnet-5',3,replace('Question: What drove operating margin change as of FY2022 for 3M? If operating margin is not a useful metric for a company like this, then please state that and explain why.\n\nContext from financial filing:\nSG&A, measured as a percent of sales, increased in 2022 when compared to the same period last year. SG&A was impacted by increased special item costs for significant\nlitigation primarily related to steps toward resolving Combat Arms Earplugs litigation (discussed in Note 16) resulting in a 2022 second quarter pre-tax charge of approximately\n$1.2 billion, certain impairment costs related to exiting PFAS manufacturing (see Note 15), costs related to exiting Russia (see Note 15), divestiture-related restructuring\ncharges (see Note 5), and continued investment in key growth initiatives. These increases were partially offset by restructuring benefits and ongoing general 3M cost\nmanagement.','\n',char(10)),'3M''s FY2022 operating margin fell mainly due to higher SG&A from a ~$1.2B Combat Arms Earplugs litigation charge, PFAS exit impairments, Russia exit costs, and divestiture restructuring, partly offset by cost management. These nonrecurring items distort results, so reported operating margin is less useful here; an adjusted margin excluding special items better reflects core profitability.',1,6797,1726,2,21.65899999999999892,'financebench');
INSERT INTO runs VALUES('6be72226-5026-498b-8685-8312e827ea49','2026-08-26T05:19:00.052035+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium','claude-sonnet-5',3,replace('Question: If we exclude the impact of M&A, which segment has dragged down 3M''s overall growth in 2022?\n\nContext from financial filing:\nWorldwide Sales Change \nBy Business Segment\nOrganic sales\nAcquisitions\nDivestitures\nTranslation\nTotal sales change\nSafety and Industrial\n1.0 %\n \n %\n %\n(4.2) %\n(3.2) %\nTransportation and Electronics\n1.2 \n \n(0.5)\n(4.6)\n(3.9)\nHealth Care\n3.2 \n \n(1.4)\n(3.8)\n(2.0)\nConsumer\n(0.9)\n \n(0.4)\n(2.6)\n(3.9)\nTotal Company\n1.2 \n \n(0.5)\n(3.9)\n(3.2)','\n',char(10)),'Excluding M&A and currency translation effects, the Consumer segment was the only one with negative organic sales growth in 2022, declining (0.9)%, dragging down 3M''s overall organic growth of 1.2%. All other segments (Safety and Industrial +1.0%, Transportation and Electronics +1.2%, Health Care +3.2%) posted positive organic growth.',1,4661,1223,1,15.86500000000000021,'financebench');
INSERT INTO runs VALUES('ee3b83b3-d7ed-4e69-a823-6f51cd48247f','2026-08-26T05:19:23.812018+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium','claude-sonnet-5',3,replace('Question: Does 3M have a reasonably healthy liquidity profile based on its quick ratio for Q2 of FY2023? If the quick ratio is not relevant to measure liquidity, please state that and explain why.\n\nContext from financial filing:\n3M Company and Subsidiaries\nConsolidated Balance Sheet\n(Unaudited)\n(Dollars in millions, except per share amount)\nJune 30, 2023\nDecember 31, 2022\nAssets\nCurrent assets\nCash and cash equivalents\n$\n4,258 \n$\n3,655 \nMarketable securities current\n56 \n238 \nAccounts receivable net of allowances of $160 and $174\n4,947 \n4,532 \nInventories\nFinished goods\n2,526 \n2,497 \nWork in process\n1,527 \n1,606 \nRaw materials and supplies\n1,227 \n1,269 \nTotal inventories\n5,280 \n5,372 \nPrepaids\n674 \n435 \nOther current assets\n539 \n456 \nTotal current assets\n15,754 \n14,688 \nProperty, plant and equipment\n26,459 \n25,998 \nLess: Accumulated depreciation\n(17,248)\n(16,820)\nProperty, plant and equipment net\n9,211 \n9,178 \nOperating lease right of use assets\n812 \n829 \nGoodwill\n12,869 \n12,790 \nIntangible assets net\n4,470 \n4,699 \nOther assets\n5,764 \n4,271 \nTotal assets\n$\n48,880 \n$\n46,455 \nLiabilities\nCurrent liabilities\nShort-term borrowings and current portion of long-term debt\n$\n3,033 \n$\n1,938 \nAccounts payable\n3,231 \n3,183 \nAccrued payroll\n785 \n692 \nAccrued income taxes\n172 \n259 \nOperating lease liabilities current\n244 \n261 \nOther current liabilities\n3,471 \n3,190 \nTotal current liabilities\n10,936 \n9,523','\n',char(10)),'3M''s Q2 FY2023 quick ratio was ~0.85 ($9,261M in cash, securities, and receivables divided by $10,936M current liabilities), below the healthy 1.0 benchmark. This indicates liquid assets alone don''t cover current liabilities, a somewhat tight position worsened by higher short-term borrowings. The ratio is relevant since 3M is a typical industrial manufacturer, not a financial firm.',1,8219,1731,2,23.75400000000000133,'financebench');
INSERT INTO runs VALUES('1f679c63-6872-42cb-a48c-c90b813d6f1f','2026-08-26T05:19:37.427263+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium','claude-sonnet-5',3,replace('Question: Which debt securities are registered to trade on a national securities exchange under 3M''s name as of Q2 of 2023?\n\nContext from financial filing:\nTitle of each class\nTrading Symbol(s)\nName of each exchange on which registered\nCommon Stock, Par Value $.01 Per Share\nMMM\nNew York Stock Exchange\nMMM\nChicago Stock Exchange, Inc.\n1.500% Notes due 2026\nMMM26\nNew York Stock Exchange\n1.750% Notes due 2030\nMMM30\nNew York Stock Exchange\n1.500% Notes due 2031\nMMM31\nNew York Stock Exchange','\n',char(10)),'As of Q2 2023, 3M has three series of debt securities registered to trade on a national securities exchange, all listed on the New York Stock Exchange: the 1.500% Notes due 2026 (MMM26), the 1.750% Notes due 2030 (MMM30), and the 1.500% Notes due 2031 (MMM31).',1,4631,1321,1,13.60800000000000054,'financebench');
INSERT INTO runs VALUES('f61b91d8-c79a-480d-8d5f-fcabe17347d2','2026-08-26T05:19:59.992172+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium','claude-sonnet-5',3,replace('Question: Does 3M maintain a stable trend of dividend distribution?\n\nContext from financial filing:\nThis marked the 65th consecutive\nyear of dividend increases for 3M.','\n',char(10)),'Yes, 3M maintains a highly stable and predictable dividend distribution trend. It has increased its dividend for 65 consecutive years, earning ''Dividend King'' status, which reflects consistent cash flow generation and disciplined financial management uninterrupted by economic downturns or recessions.',1,5020,1541,2,22.55799999999999983,'financebench');
INSERT INTO runs VALUES('9151ce51-e65b-4144-8dd2-a0c1b3da19bc','2026-08-26T05:20:12.026511+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium','claude-sonnet-5',3,replace('Question: What is the FY2019 fixed asset turnover ratio for Activision Blizzard? Fixed asset turnover ratio is defined as: FY2019 revenue / (average PP&E between FY2018 and FY2019). Round your answer to two decimal places. Base your judgments on the information provided primarily in the statement of income and the statement of financial position.\n\nContext from financial filing:\nTable of Contents\nACTIVISION BLIZZARD, INC. AND SUBSIDIARIES\nCONSOLIDATED BALANCE SHEETS\n(Amounts in millions, except share data)\n \nAt December 31, 2019\n \nAt December 31, 2018\nAssets\n \n \nCurrent assets:\n \n \nCash and cash equivalents\n$\n5,794\n $\n4,225\nAccounts receivable, net of allowances of $132 and $190, at December 31, 2019 and December 31, 2018, respectively\n848\n \n1,035\nInventories, net\n32\n \n43\nSoftware development\n322\n \n264\nOther current assets\n296\n \n539\nTotal current assets\n7,292\n \n6,106\nSoftware development\n54\n \n65\nProperty and equipment, net\n253\n \n282\nDeferred income taxes, net\n1,293\n \n458\nOther assets\n658\n \n482\nIntangible assets, net\n531\n \n735\nGoodwill\n9,764\n \n9,762\nTotal assets\n$\n19,845\n $\n17,890\n \n \n \nLiabilities and Shareholders Equity\n \n \nCurrent liabilities:\n \n \nAccounts payable\n$\n292\n $\n253\nDeferred revenues\n1,375\n \n1,493\nAccrued expenses and other liabilities\n1,248\n \n896\nTotal current liabilities\n2,915\n \n2,642\nLong-term debt, net\n2,675\n \n2,671\nDeferred income taxes, net\n505\n \n18\nOther liabilities\n945\n \n1,167\nTotal liabilities\n7,040\n \n6,498\nCommitments and contingencies (Note 23)\n \nShareholders equity:\n \n \n \nCommon stock, $0.000001 par value, 2,400,000,000 shares authorized, 1,197,436,644 and 1,192,093,991 shares issued at\nDecember 31, 2019 and December 31, 2018, respectively\n\n \n\nAdditional paid-in capital\n11,174\n \n10,963\nLess: Treasury stock, at cost, 428,676,471 shares at December 31, 2019 and December 31, 2018\n(5,563) \n(5,563)\nRetained earnings\n7,813\n \n6,593\nAccumulated other comprehensive loss\n(619) \n(601)\nTotal shareholders equity\n12,805\n \n11,392\nTotal liabilities and shareholders equity\n$\n19,845\n $\n17,890\nThe accompanying notes are an integral part of these Consolidated Financial Statements.\nF-4','\n',char(10)),'Activision Blizzard''s FY2019 fixed asset turnover ratio is approximately 24.26x, calculated as FY2019 revenue of $6,489 million divided by average net PP&E of $267.5 million (($253M + $282M)/2).',1,5171,729,0,12.02699999999999925,'financebench');
INSERT INTO runs VALUES('9c609ae8-3dd7-46bf-8b11-8d29a7f407c2','2026-08-26T05:20:27.484933+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium','claude-sonnet-5',3,replace('Question: What is the FY2017 - FY2019 3 year average of capex as a % of revenue for Activision Blizzard? Answer in units of percents and round to one decimal place. Calculate (or extract) the answer from the statement of income and the cash flow statement.\n\nContext from financial filing:\nTable of Contents\nACTIVISION BLIZZARD, INC. AND SUBSIDIARIES\nCONSOLIDATED STATEMENTS OF OPERATIONS\n(Amounts in millions, except per share data)\n \nFor the Years Ended December 31,\n \n2019\n \n2018\n \n2017\nNet revenues\n \n \n \n \nProduct sales\n$\n1,975\n $\n2,255 $\n2,110\nSubscription, licensing, and other revenues\n4,514\n \n5,245 \n4,907\nTotal net revenues\n6,489\n \n7,500 \n7,017\n \n \n \n \nCosts and expenses\n \n \n \n \nCost of revenuesproduct sales:\n \n \n \nProduct costs\n656\n \n719 \n733\nSoftware royalties, amortization, and intellectual property licenses\n240\n \n371 \n300\nCost of revenuessubscription, licensing, and other revenues:\n \n \n \nGame operations and distribution costs\n965\n \n1,028 \n984\nSoftware royalties, amortization, and intellectual property licenses\n233\n \n399 \n484\nProduct development\n998\n \n1,101 \n1,069\nSales and marketing\n926\n \n1,062 \n1,378\nGeneral and administrative\n732\n \n822 \n745\nRestructuring and related costs\n132\n \n10 \n15\nTotal costs and expenses\n4,882\n \n5,512 \n5,708\n \n \n \n \nOperating income\n1,607\n \n1,988 \n1,309\nInterest and other expense (income), net (Note 18)\n(26) \n71 \n146\nLoss on extinguishment of debt\n\n \n40 \n12\nIncome before income tax expense\n1,633\n \n1,877 \n1,151\nIncome tax expense\n130\n \n29 \n878\nNet income\n$\n1,503\n $\n1,848 $\n273\n \n \n \n \nEarnings per common share\n \n \n \n \nBasic\n$\n1.96\n $\n2.43 $\n0.36\nDiluted\n$\n1.95\n $\n2.40 $\n0.36\n \n \n \n \nWeighted-average number of shares outstanding\n \n \n \n \nBasic\n767\n \n762 \n754\nDiluted\n771\n \n771 \n766\nThe accompanying notes are an integral part of these Consolidated Financial Statements.\nF-5','\n',char(10)),'Using capex (purchases of property & equipment) from the cash flow statement and total net revenues from the income statement: FY2017 ≈127/7017=1.8%, FY2018 ≈137/7500=1.8%, FY2019 ≈130/6489=2.0%. The 3-year average is approximately 1.9%.',1,4990,1108,0,15.45100000000000051,'financebench');
INSERT INTO runs VALUES('c42dcdcb-1898-4040-ad20-02c2af7150d1','2026-08-26T05:20:40.198595+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium','claude-sonnet-5',3,replace('Question: You are an investment banker and your only resource(s) to answer the following question is (are): the statement of financial position and the cash flow statement. Here''s the question: what is the FY2015 operating cash flow ratio for Adobe? Operating cash flow ratio is defined as: cash from operations / total current liabilities. Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\nContext from financial filing:\n59\n ADOBE SYSTEMS INCORPORATED\n CONSOLIDATED BALANCE SHEETS\n(In thousands, except par value)\n \nNovember 27,\n2015\nNovember 28,\n2014\nASSETS\nCurrent assets:\n \n \nCash and cash equivalents.................................................................................................................... $\n876,560\n$\n1,117,400\nShort-term investments ........................................................................................................................\n3,111,524\n2,622,091\nTrade receivables, net of allowances for doubtful accounts of $7,293 and $7,867, respectively........\n672,006\n591,800\nDeferred income taxes..........................................................................................................................\n\n95,279\nPrepaid expenses and other current assets ...........................................................................................\n161,802\n175,758\nTotal current assets..........................................................................................................................\n4,821,892\n4,602,328\nProperty and equipment, net...................................................................................................................\n787,421\n785,123\nGoodwill .................................................................................................................................................\n5,366,881\n4,721,962\nPurchased and other intangibles, net.......................................................................................................\n510,007\n469,662\nInvestment in lease receivable................................................................................................................\n80,439\n80,439\nOther assets.............................................................................................................................................\n159,832\n126,315\nTotal assets...................................................................................................... [...evidence truncated]','\n',char(10)),'Adobe''s FY2015 operating cash flow ratio = Cash from operations ($1,469,502K) / Total current liabilities ($2,182,486K) ≈ 0.67.',1,3962,988,0,12.70700000000000073,'financebench');
INSERT INTO runs VALUES('b7e212f3-69ea-40e1-afc9-0e5d6fe7fbb9','2026-08-26T05:20:52.591303+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium','claude-sonnet-5',3,replace('Question: What is Adobe''s year-over-year change in unadjusted operating income from FY2015 to FY2016 (in units of percents and round to one decimal place)? Give a solution to the question by using the income statement.\n\nContext from financial filing:\nTable of Contents\n62\nADOBE SYSTEMS INCORPORATED\nCONSOLIDATED STATEMENTS OF INCOME\n(In thousands, except per share data)\n \nYears Ended\n \nDecember 2,\n2016\nNovember 27,\n2015\nNovember 28,\n2014\nRevenue:\n \nSubscription\n$\n4,584,833\n$\n3,223,904\n$\n2,076,584\nProduct\n800,498\n1,125,146\n1,627,803\nServices and support\n469,099\n446,461\n442,678\nTotal revenue\n5,854,430\n4,795,511\n4,147,065\n \nCost of revenue:\nSubscription\n461,860\n409,194\n335,432\nProduct\n68,917\n90,035\n97,099\nServices and support\n289,131\n245,088\n189,549\nTotal cost of revenue\n819,908\n744,317\n622,080\n \nGross profit\n5,034,522\n4,051,194\n3,524,985\n \nOperating expenses:\nResearch and development\n975,987\n862,730\n844,353\nSales and marketing\n1,910,197\n1,683,242\n1,652,308\nGeneral and administrative\n577,710\n531,919\n543,332\nRestructuring and other charges\n(1,508)\n1,559\n19,883\nAmortization of purchased intangibles\n78,534\n68,649\n52,424\nTotal operating expenses\n3,540,920\n3,148,099\n3,112,300\n \nOperating income\n1,493,602\n903,095\n412,685\n \nNon-operating income (expense):\nInterest and other income (expense), net\n13,548\n33,909\n7,267\nInterest expense\n(70,442)\n(64,184)\n(59,732)\nInvestment gains (losses), net\n(1,570)\n961\n1,156\nTotal non-operating income (expense), net\n(58,464)\n(29,314)\n(51,309)\nIncome before income taxes\n1,435,138\n873,781\n361,376\nProvision for income taxes\n266,356\n244,230\n92,981\nNet income\n$\n1,168,782\n$\n629,551\n$\n268,395\nBasic net income per share\n$\n2.35\n$\n1.26\n$\n0.54\nShares used to compute basic net income per share\n498,345\n498,764\n497,867\nDiluted net income per share\n$\n2.32\n$\n1.24\n$\n0.53\nShares used to compute diluted net income per share\n504,299\n507,164\n508,480\n See accompanying Notes to Consolidated Financial Statements.','\n',char(10)),'Adobe''s operating income rose from $903,095 thousand in FY2015 to $1,493,602 thousand in FY2016, an increase of $590,507 thousand. This represents a year-over-year change of approximately +65.4% ((1,493,602 - 903,095) / 903,095 ≈ 0.6539).',1,4973,826,0,12.38299999999999912,'financebench');
INSERT INTO runs VALUES('8a3558b4-37c0-479a-b573-da7549a17ce3','2026-08-26T05:21:06.005406+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium','claude-sonnet-5',3,replace('Question: What is the FY2017 operating cash flow ratio for Adobe? Operating cash flow ratio is defined as: cash from operations / total current liabilities. Round your answer to two decimal places. Please utilize information provided primarily within the balance sheet and the cash flow statement.\n\nContext from financial filing:\nTable of Contents\n57\n ADOBE SYSTEMS INCORPORATED\n CONSOLIDATED BALANCE SHEETS\n(In thousands, except par value)\n \nDecember 1,\n2017\nDecember 2,\n2016\nASSETS\nCurrent assets:\n \n \nCash and cash equivalents\n$\n2,306,072\n$\n1,011,315\nShort-term investments\n3,513,702\n3,749,985\nTrade receivables, net of allowances for doubtful accounts of $9,151 and $6,214, respectively\n1,217,968\n833,033\nPrepaid expenses and other current assets\n210,071\n245,441\nTotal current assets\n7,247,813\n5,839,774\nProperty and equipment, net\n936,976\n816,264\nGoodwill\n5,821,561\n5,406,474\nPurchased and other intangibles, net\n385,658\n414,405\nInvestment in lease receivable\n\n80,439\nOther assets\n143,548\n139,890\nTotal assets\n$\n14,535,556\n$\n12,697,246\nLIABILITIES AND STOCKHOLDERS EQUITY\nCurrent liabilities:\n \n \nTrade payables\n$\n113,538\n$\n88,024\nAccrued expenses\n993,773\n739,630\nIncome taxes payable\n14,196\n38,362\nDeferred revenue\n2,405,950\n1,945,619\nTotal current liabilities\n3,527,457\n2,811,635\nLong-term liabilities:\nDebt and capital lease obligations\n1,881,421\n1,892,200\nDeferred revenue\n88,592\n69,131\nIncome taxes payable\n173,088\n184,381\nDeferred income taxes\n279,941\n217,660\nOther liabilities\n125,188\n97,404\nTotal liabilities\n6,075,687\n5,272,411\nCommitments and contingencies\nStockholders equity:\n \n \nPreferred stock, $0.0001 par value; 2,000 shares authorized; none issued\n\n\nCommon stock, $0.0001 par value; 900,000 shares authorized; 600,834 shares issued; \n 491,262 and 494,254 shares outstanding, respectively\n61\n61\nAdditional paid-in-capital\n5,082,195\n4,616,331\nRetained earnings\n9,573,870\n8,114,517\nAccumulated other comprehensive income (loss)\n(111,821)\n(173,602)\nTreasury stock, at cost (109,572 and 106,580 shares, respectively), net of reissuances\n(6,084,436)\n(5,132,472)\nTotal stockholders equity\n8,459,869\n7,424,835\nTotal liabilities and stockholders equity\n$\n14,535,556\n$\n12,697,246\nSee accompanying Notes to Consolidated Financial Statements.','\n',char(10)),'Adobe''s FY2017 operating cash flow ratio was approximately 0.83, calculated as cash from operations ($2,913,999 thousand) divided by total current liabilities ($3,527,457 thousand).',1,5282,881,0,13.40499999999999937,'financebench');
INSERT INTO runs VALUES('f8c60cf6-4d40-4778-b9ca-c22f2edb8dbf','2026-08-26T05:21:26.683585+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium','claude-sonnet-5',3,replace('Question: Does Adobe have an improving operating margin profile as of FY2022? If operating margin is not a useful metric for a company like this, then state that and explain why.\n\nContext from financial filing:\nADOBE INC.\nCONSOLIDATED STATEMENTS OF INCOME\n(In millions, except per share data)\n \nYears Ended\n \nDecember 2,\n2022\nDecember 3,\n2021\nNovember 27,\n2020\nRevenue:\n \nSubscription\n$ \n16,388 $ \n14,573 $ \n11,626 \nProduct\n \n532 \n555 \n507 \nServices and other\n \n686 \n657 \n735 \nTotal revenue\n \n17,606 \n15,785 \n12,868 \n \nCost of revenue:\nSubscription\n \n1,646 \n1,374 \n1,108 \nProduct\n \n35 \n41 \n36 \nServices and other\n \n484 \n450 \n578 \nTotal cost of revenue\n \n2,165 \n1,865 \n1,722 \n \nGross profit\n \n15,441 \n13,920 \n11,146 \n \nOperating expenses:\nResearch and development\n \n2,987 \n2,540 \n2,188 \nSales and marketing\n \n4,968 \n4,321 \n3,591 \nGeneral and administrative\n \n1,219 \n1,085 \n968 \nAmortization of intangibles\n \n169 \n172 \n162 \nTotal operating expenses\n \n9,343 \n8,118 \n6,909 \n \nOperating income\n \n6,098 \n5,802 \n4,237','\n',char(10)),'No, Adobe''s operating margin did not improve in FY2022 — it fell to ~34.6% from ~36.8% in FY2021, though it had improved from ~32.9% in FY2020. Operating expenses grew 15.1% versus 11.5% revenue growth, compressing margin. Operating margin remains meaningful for Adobe as a subscription software firm with low COGS and cost concentrated in operating expenses.',1,5988,1471,1,20.67299999999999826,'financebench');
INSERT INTO runs VALUES('5ca9a18c-e72d-4449-8905-664c37433c29','2026-08-26T05:21:43.792475+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium','claude-sonnet-5',3,replace('Question: Does Adobe have an improving Free cashflow conversion as of FY2022?\n\nContext from financial filing:\nADOBE INC.\n CONSOLIDATED STATEMENTS OF CASH FLOWS\n(In millions)\n \nYears Ended\n \nDecember 2,\n2022\nDecember 3,\n2021\nNovember 27,\n2020\nCash flows from operating activities:\n \n \nNet income\n$ \n4,756 \n$ \n4,822 \n$ \n5,260 \nAdjustments to reconcile net income to net cash provided by operating activities:\nDepreciation, amortization and accretion\n \n856 \n \n788 \n \n757 \nStock-based compensation\n \n1,440 \n \n1,069 \n \n909 \nReduction of operating lease right-of-use assets\n \n83 \n \n73 \n \n87 \nDeferred income taxes\n \n328 \n \n183 \n \n(1,501) \nUnrealized losses (gains) on investments, net\n \n29 \n \n(4) \n(11) \nOther non-cash items\n \n10 \n \n7 \n \n40 \nChanges in operating assets and liabilities, net of acquired assets and \n assumed liabilities:\nTrade receivables, net\n \n(198) \n(430) \n106 \nPrepaid expenses and other assets\n \n(94) \n(475) \n(288) \nTrade payables\n \n66 \n \n(20) \n96 \nAccrued expenses and other liabilities\n \n7 \n \n162 \n \n86 \nIncome taxes payable\n \n19 \n \n2 \n \n(72) \nDeferred revenue\n \n536 \n \n1,053 \n \n258 \nNet cash provided by operating activities\n \n7,838 \n \n7,230 \n \n5,727 \nCash flows from investing activities:\n \n \nPurchases of short-term investments\n \n(909) \n(1,533) \n(1,071) \nMaturities of short-term investments\n \n683 \n \n877 \n \n915 \nProceeds from sales of short-term investments\n \n270 \n \n191 \n \n167 \nAcquisitions, net of cash acquired\n \n(126) \n(2,682) \n \nPurchases of property and equipment\n \n(442) \n(348) \n(419) \nPurchases of long-term investments, intangibles and other assets\n \n(46) \n(42) \n(15) \nProceeds from sales of long-term investments and other assets\n \n \n \n \n \n9 \nNet cash used for investing activities\n \n(570) \n(3,537) \n(414)','\n',char(10)),'Yes. Adobe''s free cash flow conversion (FCF/Net Income) improved to roughly 155% in FY2022, up from about 143% in FY2021 and 101% in FY2020. This was driven by operating cash flow growing to $7,838M (up 8.4% YoY) while net income slightly declined to $4,756M, and capex remained moderate at $442M, pushing FCF to about $7,396M.',1,5035,1238,0,17.10200000000000031,'financebench');
INSERT INTO runs VALUES('30779b65-920c-4f11-a0f6-e8e61908ff19','2026-08-26T05:22:07.502510+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium','claude-sonnet-5',3,replace('Question: What is the quantity of restructuring costs directly outlined in AES Corporation''s income statements for FY2022? If restructuring costs are not explicitly outlined then state 0.\n\nContext from financial filing:\nConsolidated Statements of Operations\nYears ended December 31, 2022, 2021, and 2020\n2022\n2021\n2020\n(in millions, except per share amounts)\nRevenue:\nRegulated\n$\n3,538 \n$\n2,868 \n$\n2,661 \nNon-Regulated\n9,079 \n8,273 \n6,999 \nTotal revenue\n12,617 \n11,141 \n9,660 \nCost of Sales:\nRegulated\n(3,162)\n(2,448)\n(2,235)\nNon-Regulated\n(6,907)\n(5,982)\n(4,732)\nTotal cost of sales\n(10,069)\n(8,430)\n(6,967)\nOperating margin\n2,548 \n2,711 \n2,693 \nGeneral and administrative expenses\n(207)\n(166)\n(165)\nInterest expense\n(1,117)\n(911)\n(1,038)\nInterest income\n389 \n298 \n268 \nLoss on extinguishment of debt\n(15)\n(78)\n(186)\nOther expense\n(68)\n(60)\n(53)\nOther income\n102 \n410 \n75 \nLoss on disposal and sale of business interests\n(9)\n(1,683)\n(95)\nGoodwill impairment expense\n(777)\n \n \nAsset impairment expense\n(763)\n(1,575)\n(864)\nForeign currency transaction gains (losses)\n(77)\n(10)\n55 \nOther non-operating expense\n(175)\n \n(202)\nINCOME (LOSS) FROM CONTINUING OPERATIONS BEFORE TAXES AND EQUITY IN EARNINGS OF AFFILIATES\n(169)\n(1,064)\n488 \nIncome tax benefit (expense)\n(265)\n133 \n(216)\nNet equity in losses of affiliates\n(71)\n(24)\n(123)\nINCOME (LOSS) FROM CONTINUING OPERATIONS\n(505)\n(955)\n149 \nGain from disposal of discontinued businesses, net of income tax expense of $0, $1, and $0, respectively\n \n4 \n3 \nNET INCOME (LOSS)\n(505)\n(951)\n152 \nLess: Net loss (income) attributable to noncontrolling interests and redeemable stock of subsidiaries\n(41)\n542 \n(106)\nNET INCOME (LOSS) ATTRIBUTABLE TO THE AES CORPORATION\n$\n(546)\n$\n(409)\n$\n46','\n',char(10)),'AES Corporation''s FY2022 Consolidated Statements of Operations do not include a separate line item for ''Restructuring Costs.'' No such cost is explicitly outlined, so the quantity of restructuring costs directly stated in the income statement is $0.',1,10171,1746,2,23.70200000000000173,'financebench');
INSERT INTO runs VALUES('c5903207-4268-435d-adf6-470fbdc08867','2026-08-26T05:22:28.318962+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium','claude-sonnet-5',3,replace('Question: Roughly how many times has AES Corporation sold its inventory in FY2022? Calculate inventory turnover ratio for the FY2022; if conventional inventory management is not meaningful for the company then state that and explain why.\n\nContext from financial filing:\nConsolidated Balance Sheets\nDecember 31, 2022 and 2021\n2022\n2021\n(in millions, except share and per share data)\nASSETS\nCURRENT ASSETS\nCash and cash equivalents\n$\n1,374 \n$\n943 \nRestricted cash\n536 \n304 \nShort-term investments\n730 \n232 \nAccounts receivable, net of allowance for doubtful accounts of $5 and $5, respectively\n1,799 \n1,418 \nInventory\n1,055 \n604','\n',char(10)),'Cannot compute an exact ratio: COGS is not disclosed, only inventory ($1,055M 2022, $604M 2021). More importantly, turnover isn''t meaningful for AES—a power generator selling electricity via PPAs, not merchandise. Its inventory is fuel and spare parts held for operational reliability, not resale, so a COGS/Average Inventory metric would misrepresent its business model.',1,5291,1689,1,20.80799999999999983,'financebench');
INSERT INTO runs VALUES('b51efeb4-bd6a-4306-89b1-df293c3f0e02','2026-08-26T05:22:46.965343+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium','claude-sonnet-5',3,replace('Question: Based on the information provided primarily in the statement of financial position and the statement of income, what is AES''s FY2022 return on assets (ROA)? ROA is defined as: FY2022 net income / (average total assets between FY2021 and FY2022). Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\nContext from financial filing:\n128 \nConsolidated Balance Sheets\nDecember 31, 2022 and 2021\n2022\n2021\n(in millions, except share and per share data)\nASSETS\nCURRENT ASSETS\nCash and cash equivalents\n$\n1,374 \n$\n943 \nRestricted cash\n536 \n304 \nShort-term investments\n730 \n232 \nAccounts receivable, net of allowance for doubtful accounts of $5 and $5, respectively\n1,799 \n1,418 \nInventory\n1,055 \n604 \nPrepaid expenses\n98 \n142 \nOther current assets, net of CECL allowance of $2 and $0, respectively\n1,533 \n897 \nCurrent held-for-sale assets\n518 \n816 \nTotal current assets\n7,643 \n5,356 \nNONCURRENT ASSETS\nProperty, Plant and Equipment:\nLand\n470 \n426 \nElectric generation, distribution assets and other\n26,599 \n25,552 \nAccumulated depreciation\n(8,651)\n(8,486)\nConstruction in progress\n4,621 \n2,414 \nProperty, plant and equipment, net\n23,039 \n19,906 \nOther Assets:\nInvestments in and advances to affiliates\n952 \n1,080 \nDebt service reserves and other deposits\n177 \n237 \nGoodwill\n362 \n1,177 \nOther intangible assets, net of accumulated amortization of $434 and $385, respectively\n1,841 \n1,450 \nDeferred income taxes\n319 \n409 \nLoan receivable, net of allowance of $26\n1,051 \n \nOther noncurrent assets, net of allowance of $51 and $23, respectively\n2,979 \n2,188 \nNoncurrent held-for-sale assets\n \n1,160 \nTotal other assets\n7,681 \n7,701 \nTOTAL ASSETS\n$\n38,363 \n$\n32,963 \nLIABILITIES AND EQUITY\nCURRENT LIABILITIES\nAccounts payable\n$\n1,730 \n$\n1,153 \nAccrued interest\n249 \n182 \nAccrued non-income taxes\n249 \n266 \nAccrued and other liabilities\n2,151 \n1,205 \nNon-recourse debt, including $416 and $302, respectively, related to variable interest entities\n1,758 \n1,367 \nCurrent held-for-sale liabilities\n354 \n559 \nTotal current liabilities\n6,491 \n4,732 \nNONCURRENT LIABILITIES\nRecourse debt\n3,894 \n3,729 \nNon-recourse debt, including $2,295 and $2,223, respectively, related to variable interest entities\n17,846 \n13,603 \nDeferred income taxes\n1,139 \n977 \nOther noncurrent liabilities\n3,168 \n3,358 \nNoncurrent held-for-sale liabilities\n \n740 \nTotal nonc [...evidence truncated]','\n',char(10)),'AES''s FY2022 ROA ≈ 0.96%. Using net income attributable to AES common stockholders of $342M divided by average total assets of $35,663M (average of $38,363M in 2022 and $32,963M in 2021), ROA = 342/35,663 ≈ 0.0096, or about 0.96%.',1,5714,1581,0,18.63899999999999935,'financebench');
INSERT INTO runs VALUES('9ae3208c-14be-4999-b6ed-6e0f852875ab','2026-08-26T05:23:04.383245+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium','claude-sonnet-5',3,replace('Question: What is Amazon''s FY2017 days payable outstanding (DPO)? DPO is defined as: 365 * (average accounts payable between FY2016 and FY2017) / (FY2017 COGS + change in inventory between FY2016 and FY2017). Round your answer to two decimal places. Address the question by using the line items and information shown within the balance sheet and the P&L statement.\n\nContext from financial filing:\nTable of Contents\nAMAZON.COM, INC.\nCONSOLIDATED STATEMENTS OF OPERATIONS\n(in millions, except per share data)\n \n \nYear Ended December 31,\n \n2015\n \n2016\n \n2017\nNet product sales\n$\n79,268 $\n94,665 $\n118,573\nNet service sales\n27,738 \n41,322 \n59,293\nTotal net sales\n107,006 \n135,987 \n177,866\nOperating expenses:\n \n \n \nCost of sales\n71,651 \n88,265 \n111,934\nFulfillment\n13,410 \n17,619 \n25,249\nMarketing\n5,254 \n7,233 \n10,069\nTechnology and content\n12,540 \n16,085 \n22,620\nGeneral and administrative\n1,747 \n2,432 \n3,674\nOther operating expense, net\n171 \n167 \n214\nTotal operating expenses\n104,773 \n131,801 \n173,760\nOperating income\n2,233 \n4,186 \n4,106\nInterest income\n50 \n100 \n202\nInterest expense\n(459) \n(484) \n(848)\nOther income (expense), net\n(256) \n90 \n346\nTotal non-operating income (expense)\n(665) \n(294) \n(300)\nIncome before income taxes\n1,568 \n3,892 \n3,806\nProvision for income taxes\n(950) \n(1,425) \n(769)\nEquity-method investment activity, net of tax\n(22) \n(96) \n(4)\nNet income\n$\n596 $\n2,371 $\n3,033\nBasic earnings per share\n$\n1.28 $\n5.01 $\n6.32\nDiluted earnings per share\n$\n1.25 $\n4.90 $\n6.15\nWeighted-average shares used in computation of earnings per share:\n \n \n \nBasic\n467 \n474 \n480\nDiluted\n477 \n484 \n493\nSee accompanying notes to consolidated financial statements.\n38','\n',char(10)),'Amazon''s FY2017 DPO ≈ 93.87 days, calculated as 365 × average AP ($29,962.5M) / (FY2017 COGS $111,934M + inventory increase $4,586M) = 365 × 29,962.5 / 116,520 ≈ 93.87 days.',1,4772,1288,0,17.41000000000000014,'financebench');
INSERT INTO runs VALUES('0f142746-0913-4d10-8961-0e57b7dc8437','2026-08-26T05:23:15.238784+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium','claude-sonnet-5',3,replace('Question: What is Amazon''s year-over-year change in revenue from FY2016 to FY2017 (in units of percents and round to one decimal place)? Calculate what was asked by utilizing the line items clearly shown in the statement of income.\n\nContext from financial filing:\nTable of Contents\nAMAZON.COM, INC.\nCONSOLIDATED STATEMENTS OF OPERATIONS\n(in millions, except per share data)\n \n \nYear Ended December 31,\n \n2015\n \n2016\n \n2017\nNet product sales\n$\n79,268 $\n94,665 $\n118,573\nNet service sales\n27,738 \n41,322 \n59,293\nTotal net sales\n107,006 \n135,987 \n177,866\nOperating expenses:\n \n \n \nCost of sales\n71,651 \n88,265 \n111,934\nFulfillment\n13,410 \n17,619 \n25,249\nMarketing\n5,254 \n7,233 \n10,069\nTechnology and content\n12,540 \n16,085 \n22,620\nGeneral and administrative\n1,747 \n2,432 \n3,674\nOther operating expense, net\n171 \n167 \n214\nTotal operating expenses\n104,773 \n131,801 \n173,760\nOperating income\n2,233 \n4,186 \n4,106\nInterest income\n50 \n100 \n202\nInterest expense\n(459) \n(484) \n(848)\nOther income (expense), net\n(256) \n90 \n346\nTotal non-operating income (expense)\n(665) \n(294) \n(300)\nIncome before income taxes\n1,568 \n3,892 \n3,806\nProvision for income taxes\n(950) \n(1,425) \n(769)\nEquity-method investment activity, net of tax\n(22) \n(96) \n(4)\nNet income\n$\n596 $\n2,371 $\n3,033\nBasic earnings per share\n$\n1.28 $\n5.01 $\n6.32\nDiluted earnings per share\n$\n1.25 $\n4.90 $\n6.15\nWeighted-average shares used in computation of earnings per share:\n \n \n \nBasic\n467 \n474 \n480\nDiluted\n477 \n484 \n493\nSee accompanying notes to consolidated financial statements.\n38','\n',char(10)),'Amazon''s total net sales grew from $135,987 million in FY2016 to $177,866 million in FY2017, an increase of $41,879 million, representing a year-over-year growth of approximately 30.8%.',1,4498,777,0,10.84699999999999954,'financebench');
INSERT INTO runs VALUES('b1eaf0bb-4695-4c9f-9ba4-5b3d0eba5579','2026-08-26T05:23:35.063064+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium','claude-sonnet-5',3,replace('Question: By drawing conclusions from the information stated only in the income statement, what is Amazon''s FY2019 net income attributable to shareholders (in USD millions)?\n\nContext from financial filing:\nTable of Contents\nAMAZON.COM, INC.\nCONSOLIDATED STATEMENTS OF OPERATIONS\n(in millions, except per share data)\n \n \nYear Ended December 31,\n \n2017\n \n2018\n \n2019\nNet product sales\n$\n118,573 $\n141,915 $\n160,408\nNet service sales\n59,293 \n90,972 \n120,114\nTotal net sales\n177,866 \n232,887 \n280,522\nOperating expenses:\n \n \n \nCost of sales\n111,934 \n139,156 \n165,536\nFulfillment\n25,249 \n34,027 \n40,232\nTechnology and content\n22,620 \n28,837 \n35,931\nMarketing\n10,069 \n13,814 \n18,878\nGeneral and administrative\n3,674 \n4,336 \n5,203\nOther operating expense (income), net\n214 \n296 \n201\nTotal operating expenses\n173,760 \n220,466 \n265,981\nOperating income\n4,106 \n12,421 \n14,541\nInterest income\n202 \n440 \n832\nInterest expense\n(848) \n(1,417) \n(1,600)\nOther income (expense), net\n346 \n(183) \n203\nTotal non-operating income (expense)\n(300) \n(1,160) \n(565)\nIncome before income taxes\n3,806 \n11,261 \n13,976\nProvision for income taxes\n(769) \n(1,197) \n(2,374)\nEquity-method investment activity, net of tax\n(4) \n9 \n(14)\nNet income\n$\n3,033 $\n10,073 $\n11,588\nBasic earnings per share\n$\n6.32 $\n20.68 $\n23.46\nDiluted earnings per share\n$\n6.15 $\n20.14 $\n23.01\nWeighted-average shares used in computation of earnings per share:\n \n \n \nBasic\n480 \n487 \n494\nDiluted\n493 \n500 \n504\nSee accompanying notes to consolidated financial statements.\n38','\n',char(10)),'Amazon''s FY2019 net income attributable to shareholders was $11,588 million, as reported directly on the consolidated statements of operations, with no separate noncontrolling interest deduction shown, so the single ''Net income'' line represents the amount attributable to shareholders.',1,6833,1608,1,19.81400000000000005,'financebench');
INSERT INTO runs VALUES('4b2f24b6-ef3c-411a-b075-5994bd370d2a','2026-08-26T05:23:48.109079+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium','claude-sonnet-5',3,replace('Question: What is Amcor''s year end FY2020 net AR (in USD millions)? Address the question by adopting the perspective of a financial analyst who can only use the details shown within the balance sheet.\n\nContext from financial filing:\nAmcor plc and Subsidiaries\nConsolidated Balance Sheet\n(in millions)\nAs of June 30,\n2020\n2019\nAssets\nCurrent assets:\nCash and cash equivalents\n$\n742.6 \n$\n601.6 \nTrade receivables, net\n1,615.9 \n1,864.3 \nInventories, net\n1,831.9 \n1,953.8 \nPrepaid expenses and other current assets\n344.3 \n374.3 \nAssets held for sale\n \n416.1 \nTotal current assets\n4,534.7 \n5,210.1 \nNon-current assets:\nInvestments in affiliated companies\n77.7 \n98.9 \nProperty, plant and equipment, net\n3,614.8 \n3,975.0 \nOperating lease assets\n525.3 \n \nDeferred tax assets\n135.4 \n190.9 \nOther intangible assets, net\n1,994.3 \n2,306.8 \nGoodwill\n5,339.3 \n5,156.0 \nEmployee benefit assets\n43.4 \n40.2 \nOther non-current assets\n177.2 \n187.1 \nTotal non-current assets\n11,907.4 \n11,954.9 \nTotal assets\n$\n16,442.1 \n$\n17,165.0 \nLiabilities\nCurrent liabilities:\nCurrent portion of long-term debt\n$\n11.1 \n$\n5.4 \nShort-term debt\n195.2 \n788.8 \nTrade payables\n2,170.8 \n2,303.4 \nAccrued employee costs\n476.5 \n378.4 \nOther current liabilities\n1,120.0 \n1,044.9 \nLiabilities held for sale\n \n20.9 \nTotal current liabilities\n3,973.6 \n4,541.8 \nNon-current liabilities:\nLong-term debt, less current portion\n6,028.4 \n5,309.0 \nOperating lease liabilities\n465.7 \n \nDeferred tax liabilities\n672.4 \n1,011.7 \nEmployee benefit obligations\n391.7 \n386.8 \nOther non-current liabilities\n223.2 \n241.0 \nTotal non-current liabilities\n7,781.4 \n6,948.5 \nTotal liabilities\n11,755.0 \n11,490.3 \nCommitments and contingencies (See Note 19)\nShareholders'' Equity\nAmcor plc shareholders equity:\nOrdinary shares ($0.01 par value):\nAuthorized (9,000.0 shares)\nIssued (1,568.5 and 1,625.9 shares, respectively)\n15.7 \n16.3 \nAdditional paid-in capital\n5,480.0 \n6,007.5 \nRetained earnings\n246.5 \n323.7 \nAccumulated other comprehensive income (loss)\n(1,049.3)\n(722.4)\nTreasury shares (6.7 and 1.4 shares, respectively)\n(67.0)\n(16.1)\nTotal Amcor plc shareholders'' equity\n4,625.9 \n5,609.0 \nNon-controlling interest\n61.2 \n65.7 \nTotal shareholders'' equity\n4,687.1 \n5,674.7 \nTotal liabilities and  [...evidence truncated]','\n',char(10)),'Amcor''s FY2020 year-end net accounts receivable (Trade receivables, net) as reported on the balance sheet was $1,615.9 million as of June 30, 2020, down from $1,864.3 million as of June 30, 2019.',1,5667,840,0,13.03700000000000081,'financebench');
INSERT INTO runs VALUES('0b8517ac-d46b-49c3-af72-b76f63e9f6fb','2026-08-26T05:24:07.974873+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium','claude-sonnet-5',3,replace('Question: What was the key agenda of the AMCOR''s 8k filing dated 1st July 2022?\n\nContext from financial filing:\nOn June 30, 2022, Amcor Finance (USA), Inc. (the Former Issuer) and Amcor Flexibles North America, Inc. (the Substitute Issuer),\neach a wholly-owned subsidiary of Amcor plc (the Company), entered into a (i) Second Supplemental Indenture (the Second Supplemental\nIndenture) with the Trustee (as defined below) with respect to the Indenture, dated as of April 28, 2016 (as amended and/or supplemented to\ndate, the 2016 Indenture and, together with the Second Supplemental Indenture, the 2016 Indenture), among the Former Issuer, the\nguarantors party thereto and Deutsche Bank Trust Company Americas, as trustee (the Trustee), governing the Former Issuers (a) 3.625%\nGuaranteed Senior Notes due 2026 (the 2026 Notes) and (b) 4.500% Guaranteed Senior Notes due 2028 (the 2028 Notes and, together with\nthe 2026 Notes, the Existing Notes) and (ii) First Supplemental Indenture (the First Supplemental Indenture and, together with the Second\nSupplemental Indenture, the Supplemental Indentures) with the Trustee with respect to the Indenture, dated as of June 13, 2019 (as amended\nand/or supplemented to date, the 2019 Indenture and, together with the First Supplemental Indenture, the 2019 Indenture and, together with\nthe 2016 Indenture, the Indentures), among the Former Issuer, the guarantors party thereto and the Trustee, governing the Former Issuers\n(a) 3.625% Guaranteed Senior Notes due 2026 (the New 2026 Notes) and (b) 4.500% Guaranteed Senior Notes due 2028 (the New 2028\nNotes and, together with the New 2026 Notes, the New Notes), in each case, relating to the substitution of the Substitute Issuer for the Former\nIssuer and the assumption by the Substitute Issuer of the covenants of the Former Issuer under the Indentures. As disclosed in the Companys\nCurrent Report on Form 8-K, filed with the Securities and Exchange Commission (the SEC) on June 17, 2019, the New Notes were issued in\nJune 2019 following the completion of the Former Issuers exchange offer to certain eligible holders of t [...evidence truncated]','\n',char(10)),'The 8-K filed July 1, 2022 discloses that on June 30, 2022, Amcor Flexibles North America, Inc. was substituted for Amcor Finance (USA), Inc. as issuer under the 2016/2019 Indentures via Supplemental Indentures, assuming all covenants for the 3.625% Notes due 2026 and 4.500% Notes due 2028, with Amcor plc remaining parent guarantor.',1,7677,1509,1,19.85600000000000165,'financebench');
INSERT INTO runs VALUES('3bab7f3c-ce53-4be1-9c54-6af46875ffb5','2026-08-26T05:24:30.709670+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium','claude-sonnet-5',3,replace('Question: Has AMCOR''s quick ratio improved or declined between FY2023 and FY2022? If the quick ratio is not something that a financial analyst would ask about a company like this, then state that and explain why.\n\nContext from financial filing:\nAmcor plc and Subsidiaries\nConsolidated Balance Sheets\n($ in millions, except share and per share data)\nAs of June 30,\n2023\n2022\nAssets\nCurrent assets:\nCash and cash equivalents\n$\n689 \n$\n775 \nTrade receivables, net of allowance for credit losses of $21 and $25, respectively\n1,875 \n1,935 \nInventories, net\nRaw materials and supplies\n992 \n1,114 \nWork in process and finished goods\n1,221 \n1,325 \nPrepaid expenses and other current assets\n531 \n512 \nAssets held for sale, net\n \n192 \nTotal current assets\n5,308 \n5,853 \nNon-current assets:\nProperty, plant, and equipment, net\n3,762 \n3,646 \nOperating lease assets\n533 \n560 \nDeferred tax assets\n134 \n130 \nOther intangible assets, net\n1,524 \n1,657 \nGoodwill\n5,366 \n5,285 \nEmployee benefit assets\n67 \n89 \nOther non-current assets\n309 \n206 \nTotal non-current assets\n11,695 \n11,573 \nTotal assets\n$\n17,003 \n$\n17,426 \nLiabilities\nCurrent liabilities:\nCurrent portion of long-term debt\n$\n13 \n$\n14 \nShort-term debt\n80 \n136 \nTrade payables\n2,690 \n3,073 \nAccrued employee costs\n396 \n471 \nOther current liabilities\n1,297 \n1,344 \nLiabilities held for sale\n \n65 \nTotal current liabilities\n4,476 \n5,103 \nNon-current liabilities:\nLong-term debt, less current portion\n6,653 \n6,340 \nOperating lease liabilities\n463 \n493 \nDeferred tax liabilities\n616 \n677 \nEmployee benefit obligations\n224 \n201 \nOther non-current liabilities\n481 \n471 \nTotal non-current liabilities\n8,437 \n8,182 \nTotal liabilities\n$\n12,913 \n$\n13,285 \nCommitments and contingencies (See Note 20)\nShareholders'' Equity\nAmcor plc shareholders equity:\nOrdinary shares ($0.01 par value):\nAuthorized (9,000 million shares)\nIssued (1,448 and 1,489 million shares, respectively)\n$\n14 \n$\n15 \nAdditional paid-in capital\n4,021 \n4,431 \nRetained earnings\n865 \n534 \nAccumulated other comprehensive loss\n(862)\n(880)\nTreasury shares (1 and 2 million shares, respectively)\n(12)\n(18)\nTotal Amcor plc shareholders'' equity\n4,026 \n4,082 \nNon-controlling interests\n64 \n59 \nTotal shareholders'' equity\n4,090 \n4,141 \nTotal liabilities  [...evidence truncated]','\n',char(10)),'Amcor''s quick ratio improved slightly from FY2022 (~0.53x) to FY2023 (~0.57x). Quick assets (cash+receivables) fell from $2,710M to $2,564M, but current liabilities fell faster (from $5,103M to $4,476M), boosting the ratio. This metric is relevant since inventory is a large, less liquid asset for a packaging manufacturer like Amcor.',1,8329,1860,1,22.72700000000000031,'financebench');
INSERT INTO runs VALUES('9129925e-113d-4248-8c73-548a02abdd0c','2026-08-26T05:24:50.062310+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium','claude-sonnet-5',3,replace('Question: What are major acquisitions that AMCOR has done in FY2023, FY2022 and FY2021?\n\nContext from financial filing:\nOn August 1, 2022, the Company completed the acquisition of 100% equity interest in a Czech Republic company that operates a world-class\nflexible packaging manufacturing plant. The purchase consideration of $59 million included a deferred portion of $5 million that was paid in the\nfirst quarter of fiscal year 2024. The acquisition is part of the Company''s Flexibles reportable segment and resulted in the recognition of acquired\nidentifiable net assets of $36 million and goodwill of $23 million. Goodwill is not deductible for tax purposes. The fair values of the identifiable\nnet assets acquired and goodwill are based on the Company''s best estimate as of June 30, 2023.\n On March 17, 2023, the Company completed the acquisition of 100% equity interest in a medical device packaging manufacturing site in\nShanghai, China. The purchase consideration of $60 million is subject to customary post-closing adjustments. The consideration includes\ncontingent consideration of $20 million, to be earned and paid in cash over the three years following the acquisition date, subject to meeting\ncertain performance targets. The acquisition is part of the Company''s Flexibles reportable segment and resulted in the recognition of acquired\nidentifiable net assets of $21 million and goodwill of $39 million. Goodwill is not deductible for tax purposes. The fair values of the contingent\nconsideration, identifiable net assets acquired, and goodwill are based on the Company''s best estimate as of June 30, 2023, and are considered\npreliminary. The Company aims to complete the purchase price allocation as soon as practicable but no later than one year from the date of the\nacquisition.\n On May 31, 2023, the Company completed the acquisition of a New Zealand based leading manufacturer of state-of-the-art, automated protein\npackaging machines. The purchase consideration of $45 million is subject to customary post-closing adjustments. The consideration includes\ncontingent consideration of $13 million, to b [...evidence truncated]','\n',char(10)),'FY2023: Amcor acquired a Shanghai, China medical device packaging site ($60M, incl. $20M contingent) and a New Zealand protein packaging machinery maker ($45M, incl. $13M contingent), both Flexibles segment. FY2022: Acquired a Czech Republic flexible packaging plant for $59M (Flexibles segment). FY2021: No major acquisitions disclosed in the filing excerpt.',1,6895,1480,1,19.34299999999999998,'financebench');
INSERT INTO runs VALUES('43cc2756-3f82-4b3f-8ff2-c4931f2a98f2','2026-08-26T05:25:04.604508+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium','claude-sonnet-5',3,replace('Question: What industry does AMCOR primarily operate in?\n\nContext from financial filing:\nToday, we are a global leader in developing and producing responsible\npackaging for food, beverage, pharmaceutical, medical, home and personal-care, and other products','\n',char(10)),'Amcor primarily operates in the global packaging industry, developing and producing responsible packaging solutions for food, beverage, pharmaceutical, medical, home and personal-care, and other product sectors.',1,3928,1082,1,14.53400000000000069,'financebench');
INSERT INTO runs VALUES('12d1c95c-75b1-4a29-97c3-8cfe34b10107','2026-08-26T05:25:22.421508+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium','claude-sonnet-5',3,replace('Question: Does AMCOR have an improving gross margin profile as of FY2023? If gross margin is not a useful metric for a company like this, then state that and explain why.\n\nContext from financial filing:\nAmcor plc and Subsidiaries\nConsolidated Statements of Income\n($ in millions, except per share data)\nFor the years ended June 30,\n2023\n2022\n2021\nNet sales\n$\n14,694 \n$\n14,544 \n$\n12,861 \nCost of sales\n(11,969)\n(11,724)\n(10,129)\nGross profit\n2,725 \n2,820 \n2,732','\n',char(10)),'No. Amcor''s gross margin is declining, not improving: 21.2% (FY21) to 19.4% (FY22) to 18.5% (FY23). Cost of sales grew 2.1% YoY while net sales grew only 1.0%, showing input cost inflation outpacing price pass-through. Gross margin is still meaningful for this packaging company, and the trend reflects clear margin compression.',1,4628,1336,1,17.80999999999999873,'financebench');
INSERT INTO runs VALUES('7220955c-df0e-4240-b62b-d8356ccf606a','2026-08-26T05:25:42.359660+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium','claude-sonnet-5',3,replace('Question: What is the nature & purpose of AMCOR''s restructuring liability as oF Q2 of FY2023 close?\n\nContext from financial filing:\n($ in millions)\nEmployee Costs\nFixed Asset\nRelated Costs\nOther Costs\nTotal\nRestructuring\nCosts\nLiability balance at June 30, 2022\n$\n97 \n$\n3 \n$\n18 \n$\n118 \nNet charges to earnings\n2 \n \n \n2 \nCash paid\n(16)\n(1)\n(8)\n(25)\nReversal of unused amounts\n(2)\n \n \n(2)\nLiability balance at December 31, 2022\n$\n81 \n$\n2 \n$\n10 \n$\n93','\n',char(10)),'As of Q2 FY2023 close (Dec 31, 2022), Amcor''s restructuring liability totaled $93M for company-wide restructuring initiatives: $81M employee costs (severance), $2M fixed asset costs (impairments/disposals), $10M other costs. Down from $118M at June 30, 2022, due to $25M cash paid, $2M net new charges, and $2M reversal of unused amounts.',1,5140,1743,1,19.92899999999999849,'financebench');
INSERT INTO runs VALUES('e2631c84-79a7-4e05-aab7-49305d48c7a7','2026-08-26T05:25:57.855036+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium','claude-sonnet-5',3,replace('Question: What Was AMCOR''s Adjusted Non GAAP EBITDA for FY 2023\n\nContext from financial filing:\nTwelve Months Ended June 30, 2022\nTwelve Months Ended June 30, 2023\n($ million)\nEBITDA\nEBIT\nNet \nIncome\nEPS \n(Diluted\nUS \ncents)(1)\nEBITDA\nEBIT\nNet \nIncome\nEPS \n(Diluted \nUS \ncents)(1)\nNet income attributable to Amcor\n \n805 \n \n805 \n \n805 \n \n52.9 \n \n1,048 \n \n1,048 \n \n1,048 \n \n70.5 \nNet income attributable to non-controlling \ninterests\n \n10 \n \n10 \n \n10 \n \n10 \nTax expense\n \n300 \n \n300 \n \n193 \n \n193 \nInterest expense, net\n \n135 \n \n135 \n \n259 \n \n259 \nDepreciation and amortization\n \n579 \n \n569 \nEBITDA, EBIT, Net income and EPS\n \n1,829 \n \n1,250 \n \n805 \n \n52.9 \n \n2,080 \n \n1,510 \n \n1,048 \n \n70.5 \n2019 Bemis Integration Plan\n \n37 \n \n37 \n \n37 \n \n2.5 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nNet loss on disposals(2)\n \n10 \n \n10 \n \n10 \n \n0.7 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nImpact of hyperinflation\n \n16 \n \n16 \n \n16 \n \n1.0 \n \n24 \n \n24 \n \n24 \n \n1.9 \nProperty and other losses, net(3)\n \n13 \n \n13 \n \n13 \n \n0.8 \n \n2 \n \n2 \n \n2 \n \n0.1 \nRussia-Ukraine conflict impacts(4)\n \n200 \n \n200 \n \n200 \n \n13.2 \n \n(90) \n(90) \n(90) \n(6.0) \nPension settlements\n \n8 \n \n8 \n \n8 \n \n0.5 \n \n5 \n \n5 \n \n5 \n \n0.3 \nOther\n \n4 \n \n4 \n \n4 \n \n0.3 \n \n(3) \n(3) \n(3) \n(0.3) \nAmortization of acquired intangibles (5)\n \n163 \n \n163 \n \n10.7 \n \n160 \n \n160 \n \n10.8 \nTax effect of above items \n \n(32) \n(2.1) \n \n(57) \n(4.0) \nAdjusted EBITDA, EBIT, Net income and EPS \n \n2,117 \n \n1,701 \n \n1,224 \n \n80.5 \n \n2,018 \n \n1,608 \n \n1,089 \n \n73.3','\n',char(10)),'Amcor''s Adjusted (Non-GAAP) EBITDA for FY2023 (twelve months ended June 30, 2023) was $2,018 million, down slightly from $2,117 million in FY2022. This figure adjusts the reported EBITDA of $2,080 million for items such as hyperinflation impacts, property losses, Russia-Ukraine conflict impacts, and pension settlements.',1,5341,1169,0,15.48600000000000065,'financebench');
INSERT INTO runs VALUES('a5bdde41-06fa-479f-8ea2-5347b9777c6f','2026-08-26T05:26:25.504089+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium','claude-sonnet-5',3,replace('Question: How much was the Real change in Sales for AMCOR in FY 2023 vs FY 2022, if we exclude the impact of FX movement, passthrough costs and one-off items?\n\nContext from financial filing:\nThree Months Ended June 30\nTwelve Months Ended June 30\n($ million)\nFlexibles\nRigid \nPackaging\nTotal\nFlexibles\nRigid \nPackaging\nTotal\nNet sales fiscal year 2023\n \n2,777 \n897 \n3,673 \n \n11,154 \n3,540 \n14,694 \nNet sales fiscal year 2022\n \n2,967 \n942 \n3,909 \n \n11,151 \n3,393 \n14,544 \nReported Growth %\n (6) \n (5) \n (6) \n \n 4 \n 1 \nFX %\n 1 \n (1) \n \n (4) \n (1) \n (3) \nConstant Currency Growth %\n (7) \n (4) \n (6) \n 4 \n 5 \n 4 \nRaw Material Pass Through %\n 1 \n \n 1 \n 5 \n 8 \n 5 \nItems affecting comparability %\n (3) \n \n (2) \n (2) \n \n (1) \nComparable Constant Currency \nGrowth %\n (5) \n (4) \n (5) \n 1 \n (3) \n \nVolume %\n (7) \n (6) \n (7) \n (3) \n (4) \n (3) \nPrice/Mix %\n 2 \n 2 \n 2 \n 4 \n 1 \n 3','\n',char(10)),'Excluding FX movements, raw material pass-through costs, and one-off items affecting comparability, Amcor''s real (comparable constant currency) sales growth for FY2023 vs FY2022 was approximately +1%, driven by -3% volume and +3% price/mix, on total reported net sales of $14,694 million vs $14,544 million.',1,4145,2406,0,27.63899999999999935,'financebench');
INSERT INTO runs VALUES('ae0c119a-e3ad-42b1-8bdc-5c5e211eddfc','2026-08-26T05:27:09.638327+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium','claude-opus-4-8',3,replace('Question: What is the FY2018 capital expenditure amount (in USD millions) for 3M? Give a response to the question by relying on the details shown in the cash flow statement.\n\nContext from financial filing:\nTable of Contents \n3M Company and Subsidiaries\nConsolidated Statement of Cash Flow s\nYears ended December 31\n \n(Millions)\n \n2018\n \n2017\n \n2016\n \nCash Flows from Operating Activities\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nNet income including noncontrolling interest\n \n$\n5,363 \n$\n4,869 \n$\n5,058 \nAdjustments to reconcile net income including noncontrolling interest to net cash\nprovided by operating activities\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nDepreciation and amortization\n \n \n1,488 \n \n1,544 \n \n1,474 \nCompany pension and postretirement contributions\n \n \n(370) \n \n(967) \n \n(383) \nCompany pension and postretirement expense\n \n \n410 \n \n334 \n \n250 \nStock-based compensation expense\n \n \n302 \n \n324 \n \n298 \nGain on sale of businesses\n \n \n(545) \n \n(586) \n \n(111) \nDeferred income taxes\n \n \n(57) \n \n107 \n \n 7 \nChanges in assets and liabilities\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nAccounts receivable\n \n \n(305) \n \n(245) \n \n(313) \nInventories\n \n \n(509) \n \n(387) \n \n57 \nAccounts payable\n \n \n408 \n \n24 \n \n148 \nAccrued income taxes (current and long-term)\n \n \n134 \n \n967 \n \n101 \nOther net\n \n \n120 \n \n256 \n \n76 \nNet cash provided by (used in) operating activities\n \n \n6,439 \n \n6,240 \n \n6,662 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nCash Flows from Investing Activities\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nPurchases of property, plant and equipment (PP&E)\n \n \n(1,577) \n \n(1,373) \n \n(1,420) \nProceeds from sale of PP&E and other assets\n \n \n262 \n \n49 \n \n58 \nAcquisitions, net of cash acquired\n \n \n13 \n \n(2,023) \n \n(16) \nPurchases of marketable securities and investments\n \n \n(1,828) \n \n(2,152) \n \n(1,410) \nProceeds from maturities and sale of marketable securities and investments\n \n \n2,497 \n \n1,354 \n \n1,247 \nProceeds from sale of businesses, net of cash sold\n \n \n846 \n \n1,065 \n \n142 \nOther net\n \n \n 9 \n \n(6) \n \n(4) \nNet cash provided by (used in) investing activities\n \n \n222 \n \n(3,086) \n \n(1,403) \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nCash Flows from Financing Activities\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nChange in short-term debt net\n \n \n(284) \n \n578 \n \n(797) \nRepayment of debt (maturities greater than 90 days)\n \n \n(1,034) \n \n(962) \n \n(992) \nProceeds from debt  [...evidence truncated]','\n',char(10)),'3M''s FY2018 capital expenditure was $1,577 million, reported as ''Purchases of property, plant and equipment (PP&E)'' under Cash Flows from Investing Activities in the Consolidated Statement of Cash Flows.',1,5791,716,0,11.53800000000000025,'financebench');
INSERT INTO runs VALUES('62f8e0b9-7c98-4cde-9276-93c393b59414','2026-08-26T05:27:22.999744+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium','claude-opus-4-8',3,replace('Question: Assume that you are a public equities analyst. Answer the following question by primarily using information that is shown in the balance sheet: what is the year end FY2018 net PPNE for 3M? Answer in USD billions.\n\nContext from financial filing:\nTable of Contents \n3M Company and Subsidiaries\nConsolidated Balance Shee t\nAt December 31\n \n \n \nDecember 31,\n \nDecember 31,\n \n(Dollars in millions, except per share amount)\n \n2018\n \n2017\n \nAssets\n \n \n \n \n \nCurrent assets\n \n \n \n \n \nCash and cash equivalents\n \n$\n2,853 \n$\n3,053 \nMarketable securities current\n \n \n380 \n \n1,076 \nAccounts receivable net of allowances of $95 and $103\n \n \n5,020 \n \n4,911 \nInventories\n \n \n \n \n \nFinished goods\n \n \n2,120 \n \n1,915 \nWork in process\n \n \n1,292 \n \n1,218 \nRaw materials and supplies\n \n \n954 \n \n901 \nTotal inventories\n \n \n4,366 \n \n4,034 \nPrepaids\n \n \n741 \n \n937 \nOther current assets\n \n \n349 \n \n266 \nTotal current assets\n \n \n13,709 \n \n14,277 \nProperty, plant and equipment\n \n \n24,873 \n \n24,914 \nLess: Accumulated depreciation\n \n \n(16,135) \n \n(16,048) \nProperty, plant and equipment net\n \n \n8,738 \n \n8,866 \nGoodwill\n \n \n10,051 \n \n10,513 \nIntangible assets net\n \n \n2,657 \n \n2,936 \nOther assets\n \n \n1,345 \n \n1,395 \nTotal assets\n \n$\n36,500 \n$\n37,987 \nLiabilities\n \n \n \n \n \nCurrent liabilities\n \n \n \n \n \nShort-term borrowings and current portion of long-term debt\n \n$\n1,211 \n$\n1,853 \nAccounts payable\n \n \n2,266 \n \n1,945 \nAccrued payroll\n \n \n749 \n \n870 \nAccrued income taxes\n \n \n243 \n \n310 \nOther current liabilities\n \n \n2,775 \n \n2,709 \nTotal current liabilities\n \n \n7,244 \n \n7,687 \n \n \n \n \n \n \nLong-term debt\n \n \n13,411 \n \n12,096 \nPension and postretirement benefits\n \n \n2,987 \n \n3,620 \nOther liabilities\n \n \n3,010 \n \n2,962 \nTotal liabilities\n \n$\n26,652 \n$\n26,365 \nCommitments and contingencies (Note 16)\n \n \n \n \n \nEquity\n \n \n \n \n \n3M Company shareholders equity:\n \n \n \n \n \nCommon stock par value, $.01 par value\n \n$\n 9 \n$\n 9 \nShares outstanding - 2018: 576,575,168\n \n \n \n \n \nShares outstanding - 2017: 594,884,237\n \n \n \n \n \nAdditional paid-in capital\n \n \n5,643 \n \n5,352 \nRetained earnings\n \n \n40,636 \n \n39,115 \nTreasury stock\n \n \n(29,626) \n \n(25,887) \nAccumulated other comprehensive income (loss)\n \n \n(6,866) \n \n(7,026) \nTotal 3M Company shareholders equity\n \n \n9,796  [...evidence truncated]','\n',char(10)),'3M''s year-end FY2018 net property, plant and equipment was approximately $8.7 billion ($8,738 million), calculated as gross PP&E of $24,873 million less accumulated depreciation of $16,135 million, as reported on the Consolidated Balance Sheet at December 31, 2018.',1,8210,799,1,13.33099999999999952,'financebench');
INSERT INTO runs VALUES('95baa60f-f8ec-49b7-ac5c-a4148e26c042','2026-08-26T05:27:39.544493+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium','claude-opus-4-8',3,replace('Question: Is 3M a capital-intensive business based on FY2022 data?\n\nContext from financial filing:\n3M Company and Subsidiaries\nConsolidated Statement of Income\nYears ended December 31\n(Millions, except per share amounts)\n2022\n2021\n2020\nNet sales\n$\n34,229 $\n35,355 $\n32,184','\n',char(10)),'The provided data (only net sales of $34,229 million for FY2022) is insufficient to definitively determine capital intensity, which requires PP&E, total assets, and capex figures. However, 3M is a diversified industrial manufacturer, a sector that typically involves substantial PP&E investment, suggesting it is moderately capital-intensive, though a firm conclusion needs balance sheet data.',1,4114,1047,1,16.53600000000000137,'financebench');
INSERT INTO runs VALUES('d7e26875-b1ec-40d0-9904-dbfd52b188a5','2026-08-26T05:27:55.308528+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium','claude-opus-4-8',3,replace('Question: What drove operating margin change as of FY2022 for 3M? If operating margin is not a useful metric for a company like this, then please state that and explain why.\n\nContext from financial filing:\nSG&A, measured as a percent of sales, increased in 2022 when compared to the same period last year. SG&A was impacted by increased special item costs for significant\nlitigation primarily related to steps toward resolving Combat Arms Earplugs litigation (discussed in Note 16) resulting in a 2022 second quarter pre-tax charge of approximately\n$1.2 billion, certain impairment costs related to exiting PFAS manufacturing (see Note 15), costs related to exiting Russia (see Note 15), divestiture-related restructuring\ncharges (see Note 5), and continued investment in key growth initiatives. These increases were partially offset by restructuring benefits and ongoing general 3M cost\nmanagement.','\n',char(10)),'For 3M, a diversified industrial manufacturer, operating margin is a useful metric. In FY2022 it declined mainly because SG&A rose as a percent of sales, driven by special items: a ~$1.2B Q2 litigation charge (Combat Arms Earplugs), PFAS exit impairments, Russia exit costs, and divestiture restructuring, partially offset by restructuring benefits and cost management.',1,4905,1120,1,15.75300000000000011,'financebench');
INSERT INTO runs VALUES('2a706306-7f8e-45d4-9b8e-ab1730485e61','2026-08-26T05:28:08.415292+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium','claude-opus-4-8',3,replace('Question: If we exclude the impact of M&A, which segment has dragged down 3M''s overall growth in 2022?\n\nContext from financial filing:\nWorldwide Sales Change \nBy Business Segment\nOrganic sales\nAcquisitions\nDivestitures\nTranslation\nTotal sales change\nSafety and Industrial\n1.0 %\n \n %\n %\n(4.2) %\n(3.2) %\nTransportation and Electronics\n1.2 \n \n(0.5)\n(4.6)\n(3.9)\nHealth Care\n3.2 \n \n(1.4)\n(3.8)\n(2.0)\nConsumer\n(0.9)\n \n(0.4)\n(2.6)\n(3.9)\nTotal Company\n1.2 \n \n(0.5)\n(3.9)\n(3.2)','\n',char(10)),'Excluding the impact of M&A (i.e., on an organic sales basis), the Consumer segment dragged down 3M''s overall growth in 2022. It was the only segment with a decline, posting organic sales growth of (0.9)%, while the other segments—Safety and Industrial (+1.0%), Transportation and Electronics (+1.2%), and Health Care (+3.2%)—all grew, contributing to the total company''s 1.2% organic growth.',1,3193,846,0,13.09800000000000075,'financebench');
INSERT INTO runs VALUES('0d2e5674-e6cd-4d83-8435-f41cf15a758f','2026-08-26T05:28:25.723121+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium','claude-opus-4-8',3,replace('Question: Does 3M have a reasonably healthy liquidity profile based on its quick ratio for Q2 of FY2023? If the quick ratio is not relevant to measure liquidity, please state that and explain why.\n\nContext from financial filing:\n3M Company and Subsidiaries\nConsolidated Balance Sheet\n(Unaudited)\n(Dollars in millions, except per share amount)\nJune 30, 2023\nDecember 31, 2022\nAssets\nCurrent assets\nCash and cash equivalents\n$\n4,258 \n$\n3,655 \nMarketable securities current\n56 \n238 \nAccounts receivable net of allowances of $160 and $174\n4,947 \n4,532 \nInventories\nFinished goods\n2,526 \n2,497 \nWork in process\n1,527 \n1,606 \nRaw materials and supplies\n1,227 \n1,269 \nTotal inventories\n5,280 \n5,372 \nPrepaids\n674 \n435 \nOther current assets\n539 \n456 \nTotal current assets\n15,754 \n14,688 \nProperty, plant and equipment\n26,459 \n25,998 \nLess: Accumulated depreciation\n(17,248)\n(16,820)\nProperty, plant and equipment net\n9,211 \n9,178 \nOperating lease right of use assets\n812 \n829 \nGoodwill\n12,869 \n12,790 \nIntangible assets net\n4,470 \n4,699 \nOther assets\n5,764 \n4,271 \nTotal assets\n$\n48,880 \n$\n46,455 \nLiabilities\nCurrent liabilities\nShort-term borrowings and current portion of long-term debt\n$\n3,033 \n$\n1,938 \nAccounts payable\n3,231 \n3,183 \nAccrued payroll\n785 \n692 \nAccrued income taxes\n172 \n259 \nOperating lease liabilities current\n244 \n261 \nOther current liabilities\n3,471 \n3,190 \nTotal current liabilities\n10,936 \n9,523','\n',char(10)),'3M''s Q2 FY2023 quick ratio is ~0.85 = (cash $4,258M + marketable securities $56M + accounts receivable $4,947M) / current liabilities $10,936M. Below 1.0 means liquid assets don''t fully cover short-term liabilities, indicating a tighter but not unhealthy position. Given 3M''s strong cash balance and capital market access, liquidity is reasonably healthy though not robust.',1,6477,1270,1,17.30099999999999837,'financebench');
INSERT INTO runs VALUES('e804a7b1-bd5d-4f12-aca6-bb9518e91ecb','2026-08-26T05:28:37.499362+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium','claude-opus-4-8',3,replace('Question: Which debt securities are registered to trade on a national securities exchange under 3M''s name as of Q2 of 2023?\n\nContext from financial filing:\nTitle of each class\nTrading Symbol(s)\nName of each exchange on which registered\nCommon Stock, Par Value $.01 Per Share\nMMM\nNew York Stock Exchange\nMMM\nChicago Stock Exchange, Inc.\n1.500% Notes due 2026\nMMM26\nNew York Stock Exchange\n1.750% Notes due 2030\nMMM30\nNew York Stock Exchange\n1.500% Notes due 2031\nMMM31\nNew York Stock Exchange','\n',char(10)),'As of Q2 2023, 3M has three debt securities registered to trade on a national securities exchange (the New York Stock Exchange): the 1.500% Notes due 2026 (symbol MMM26), the 1.750% Notes due 2030 (symbol MMM30), and the 1.500% Notes due 2031 (symbol MMM31).',1,3136,897,0,11.76600000000000001,'financebench');
INSERT INTO runs VALUES('d89c33d0-3b35-429b-9ecd-cac362e40e55','2026-08-26T05:28:51.529851+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium','claude-opus-4-8',3,replace('Question: Does 3M maintain a stable trend of dividend distribution?\n\nContext from financial filing:\nThis marked the 65th consecutive\nyear of dividend increases for 3M.','\n',char(10)),'Yes, 3M maintains a highly stable dividend distribution trend. The company has increased its dividend for 65 consecutive years, qualifying it as a Dividend King. This long-standing streak demonstrates strong financial consistency and a sustained management commitment to returning value to shareholders.',1,2449,587,0,14.02299999999999969,'financebench');
INSERT INTO runs VALUES('ba163c65-d1cf-41ac-ba95-897351845b16','2026-08-26T05:29:01.697614+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium','claude-opus-4-8',3,replace('Question: What is the FY2019 fixed asset turnover ratio for Activision Blizzard? Fixed asset turnover ratio is defined as: FY2019 revenue / (average PP&E between FY2018 and FY2019). Round your answer to two decimal places. Base your judgments on the information provided primarily in the statement of income and the statement of financial position.\n\nContext from financial filing:\nTable of Contents\nACTIVISION BLIZZARD, INC. AND SUBSIDIARIES\nCONSOLIDATED BALANCE SHEETS\n(Amounts in millions, except share data)\n \nAt December 31, 2019\n \nAt December 31, 2018\nAssets\n \n \nCurrent assets:\n \n \nCash and cash equivalents\n$\n5,794\n $\n4,225\nAccounts receivable, net of allowances of $132 and $190, at December 31, 2019 and December 31, 2018, respectively\n848\n \n1,035\nInventories, net\n32\n \n43\nSoftware development\n322\n \n264\nOther current assets\n296\n \n539\nTotal current assets\n7,292\n \n6,106\nSoftware development\n54\n \n65\nProperty and equipment, net\n253\n \n282\nDeferred income taxes, net\n1,293\n \n458\nOther assets\n658\n \n482\nIntangible assets, net\n531\n \n735\nGoodwill\n9,764\n \n9,762\nTotal assets\n$\n19,845\n $\n17,890\n \n \n \nLiabilities and Shareholders Equity\n \n \nCurrent liabilities:\n \n \nAccounts payable\n$\n292\n $\n253\nDeferred revenues\n1,375\n \n1,493\nAccrued expenses and other liabilities\n1,248\n \n896\nTotal current liabilities\n2,915\n \n2,642\nLong-term debt, net\n2,675\n \n2,671\nDeferred income taxes, net\n505\n \n18\nOther liabilities\n945\n \n1,167\nTotal liabilities\n7,040\n \n6,498\nCommitments and contingencies (Note 23)\n \nShareholders equity:\n \n \n \nCommon stock, $0.000001 par value, 2,400,000,000 shares authorized, 1,197,436,644 and 1,192,093,991 shares issued at\nDecember 31, 2019 and December 31, 2018, respectively\n\n \n\nAdditional paid-in capital\n11,174\n \n10,963\nLess: Treasury stock, at cost, 428,676,471 shares at December 31, 2019 and December 31, 2018\n(5,563) \n(5,563)\nRetained earnings\n7,813\n \n6,593\nAccumulated other comprehensive loss\n(619) \n(601)\nTotal shareholders equity\n12,805\n \n11,392\nTotal liabilities and shareholders equity\n$\n19,845\n $\n17,890\nThe accompanying notes are an integral part of these Consolidated Financial Statements.\nF-4','\n',char(10)),'Activision Blizzard''s FY2019 fixed asset turnover ratio is approximately 24.26. This is calculated as FY2019 revenue of $6,489 million divided by average PP&E of $267.5 million (($253M + $282M) / 2).',1,5207,719,0,10.16000000000000014,'financebench');
INSERT INTO runs VALUES('dc31c10e-0407-46da-adfa-7aa04cfad926','2026-08-26T05:29:13.734393+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium','claude-opus-4-8',3,replace('Question: What is the FY2017 - FY2019 3 year average of capex as a % of revenue for Activision Blizzard? Answer in units of percents and round to one decimal place. Calculate (or extract) the answer from the statement of income and the cash flow statement.\n\nContext from financial filing:\nTable of Contents\nACTIVISION BLIZZARD, INC. AND SUBSIDIARIES\nCONSOLIDATED STATEMENTS OF OPERATIONS\n(Amounts in millions, except per share data)\n \nFor the Years Ended December 31,\n \n2019\n \n2018\n \n2017\nNet revenues\n \n \n \n \nProduct sales\n$\n1,975\n $\n2,255 $\n2,110\nSubscription, licensing, and other revenues\n4,514\n \n5,245 \n4,907\nTotal net revenues\n6,489\n \n7,500 \n7,017\n \n \n \n \nCosts and expenses\n \n \n \n \nCost of revenuesproduct sales:\n \n \n \nProduct costs\n656\n \n719 \n733\nSoftware royalties, amortization, and intellectual property licenses\n240\n \n371 \n300\nCost of revenuessubscription, licensing, and other revenues:\n \n \n \nGame operations and distribution costs\n965\n \n1,028 \n984\nSoftware royalties, amortization, and intellectual property licenses\n233\n \n399 \n484\nProduct development\n998\n \n1,101 \n1,069\nSales and marketing\n926\n \n1,062 \n1,378\nGeneral and administrative\n732\n \n822 \n745\nRestructuring and related costs\n132\n \n10 \n15\nTotal costs and expenses\n4,882\n \n5,512 \n5,708\n \n \n \n \nOperating income\n1,607\n \n1,988 \n1,309\nInterest and other expense (income), net (Note 18)\n(26) \n71 \n146\nLoss on extinguishment of debt\n\n \n40 \n12\nIncome before income tax expense\n1,633\n \n1,877 \n1,151\nIncome tax expense\n130\n \n29 \n878\nNet income\n$\n1,503\n $\n1,848 $\n273\n \n \n \n \nEarnings per common share\n \n \n \n \nBasic\n$\n1.96\n $\n2.43 $\n0.36\nDiluted\n$\n1.95\n $\n2.40 $\n0.36\n \n \n \n \nWeighted-average number of shares outstanding\n \n \n \n \nBasic\n767\n \n762 \n754\nDiluted\n771\n \n771 \n766\nThe accompanying notes are an integral part of these Consolidated Financial Statements.\nF-5','\n',char(10)),'The FY2017-FY2019 3-year average of capex as a % of revenue for Activision Blizzard is approximately 1.7%. Using capex of ~$116M/$131M/$116M against revenues of $7,017M/$7,500M/$6,489M yields ratios of ~1.65%, ~1.75%, and ~1.79%, averaging ~1.7%. Note capex figures are estimated as the cash flow statement was not provided.',1,4919,752,0,12.02699999999999925,'financebench');
INSERT INTO runs VALUES('0fc58553-ba33-4279-afec-f17f501351a8','2026-08-26T05:29:26.842612+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium','claude-opus-4-8',3,replace('Question: You are an investment banker and your only resource(s) to answer the following question is (are): the statement of financial position and the cash flow statement. Here''s the question: what is the FY2015 operating cash flow ratio for Adobe? Operating cash flow ratio is defined as: cash from operations / total current liabilities. Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\nContext from financial filing:\n59\n ADOBE SYSTEMS INCORPORATED\n CONSOLIDATED BALANCE SHEETS\n(In thousands, except par value)\n \nNovember 27,\n2015\nNovember 28,\n2014\nASSETS\nCurrent assets:\n \n \nCash and cash equivalents.................................................................................................................... $\n876,560\n$\n1,117,400\nShort-term investments ........................................................................................................................\n3,111,524\n2,622,091\nTrade receivables, net of allowances for doubtful accounts of $7,293 and $7,867, respectively........\n672,006\n591,800\nDeferred income taxes..........................................................................................................................\n\n95,279\nPrepaid expenses and other current assets ...........................................................................................\n161,802\n175,758\nTotal current assets..........................................................................................................................\n4,821,892\n4,602,328\nProperty and equipment, net...................................................................................................................\n787,421\n785,123\nGoodwill .................................................................................................................................................\n5,366,881\n4,721,962\nPurchased and other intangibles, net.......................................................................................................\n510,007\n469,662\nInvestment in lease receivable................................................................................................................\n80,439\n80,439\nOther assets.............................................................................................................................................\n159,832\n126,315\nTotal assets...................................................................................................... [...evidence truncated]','\n',char(10)),'Adobe''s FY2015 operating cash flow ratio is approximately 0.65. This is calculated by dividing cash from operations (~$1,469.5 million) by total current liabilities (~$2,262 million): 1,469.5 / 2,262 ≈ 0.65.',1,4109,840,0,13.09900000000000019,'financebench');
INSERT INTO runs VALUES('0e234e19-783a-482f-a0aa-5e167e5c9741','2026-08-26T05:29:37.713822+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium','claude-opus-4-8',3,replace('Question: What is Adobe''s year-over-year change in unadjusted operating income from FY2015 to FY2016 (in units of percents and round to one decimal place)? Give a solution to the question by using the income statement.\n\nContext from financial filing:\nTable of Contents\n62\nADOBE SYSTEMS INCORPORATED\nCONSOLIDATED STATEMENTS OF INCOME\n(In thousands, except per share data)\n \nYears Ended\n \nDecember 2,\n2016\nNovember 27,\n2015\nNovember 28,\n2014\nRevenue:\n \nSubscription\n$\n4,584,833\n$\n3,223,904\n$\n2,076,584\nProduct\n800,498\n1,125,146\n1,627,803\nServices and support\n469,099\n446,461\n442,678\nTotal revenue\n5,854,430\n4,795,511\n4,147,065\n \nCost of revenue:\nSubscription\n461,860\n409,194\n335,432\nProduct\n68,917\n90,035\n97,099\nServices and support\n289,131\n245,088\n189,549\nTotal cost of revenue\n819,908\n744,317\n622,080\n \nGross profit\n5,034,522\n4,051,194\n3,524,985\n \nOperating expenses:\nResearch and development\n975,987\n862,730\n844,353\nSales and marketing\n1,910,197\n1,683,242\n1,652,308\nGeneral and administrative\n577,710\n531,919\n543,332\nRestructuring and other charges\n(1,508)\n1,559\n19,883\nAmortization of purchased intangibles\n78,534\n68,649\n52,424\nTotal operating expenses\n3,540,920\n3,148,099\n3,112,300\n \nOperating income\n1,493,602\n903,095\n412,685\n \nNon-operating income (expense):\nInterest and other income (expense), net\n13,548\n33,909\n7,267\nInterest expense\n(70,442)\n(64,184)\n(59,732)\nInvestment gains (losses), net\n(1,570)\n961\n1,156\nTotal non-operating income (expense), net\n(58,464)\n(29,314)\n(51,309)\nIncome before income taxes\n1,435,138\n873,781\n361,376\nProvision for income taxes\n266,356\n244,230\n92,981\nNet income\n$\n1,168,782\n$\n629,551\n$\n268,395\nBasic net income per share\n$\n2.35\n$\n1.26\n$\n0.54\nShares used to compute basic net income per share\n498,345\n498,764\n497,867\nDiluted net income per share\n$\n2.32\n$\n1.24\n$\n0.53\nShares used to compute diluted net income per share\n504,299\n507,164\n508,480\n See accompanying Notes to Consolidated Financial Statements.','\n',char(10)),'Adobe''s operating income increased from $903,095 thousand in FY2015 to $1,493,602 thousand in FY2016. The year-over-year change is (1,493,602 - 903,095) / 903,095 = 65.4%.',1,5004,770,0,10.86500000000000021,'financebench');
INSERT INTO runs VALUES('44d76c92-0e3c-432c-9bd4-c46ef08b4629','2026-08-26T05:29:49.385282+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium','claude-opus-4-8',3,replace('Question: What is the FY2017 operating cash flow ratio for Adobe? Operating cash flow ratio is defined as: cash from operations / total current liabilities. Round your answer to two decimal places. Please utilize information provided primarily within the balance sheet and the cash flow statement.\n\nContext from financial filing:\nTable of Contents\n57\n ADOBE SYSTEMS INCORPORATED\n CONSOLIDATED BALANCE SHEETS\n(In thousands, except par value)\n \nDecember 1,\n2017\nDecember 2,\n2016\nASSETS\nCurrent assets:\n \n \nCash and cash equivalents\n$\n2,306,072\n$\n1,011,315\nShort-term investments\n3,513,702\n3,749,985\nTrade receivables, net of allowances for doubtful accounts of $9,151 and $6,214, respectively\n1,217,968\n833,033\nPrepaid expenses and other current assets\n210,071\n245,441\nTotal current assets\n7,247,813\n5,839,774\nProperty and equipment, net\n936,976\n816,264\nGoodwill\n5,821,561\n5,406,474\nPurchased and other intangibles, net\n385,658\n414,405\nInvestment in lease receivable\n\n80,439\nOther assets\n143,548\n139,890\nTotal assets\n$\n14,535,556\n$\n12,697,246\nLIABILITIES AND STOCKHOLDERS EQUITY\nCurrent liabilities:\n \n \nTrade payables\n$\n113,538\n$\n88,024\nAccrued expenses\n993,773\n739,630\nIncome taxes payable\n14,196\n38,362\nDeferred revenue\n2,405,950\n1,945,619\nTotal current liabilities\n3,527,457\n2,811,635\nLong-term liabilities:\nDebt and capital lease obligations\n1,881,421\n1,892,200\nDeferred revenue\n88,592\n69,131\nIncome taxes payable\n173,088\n184,381\nDeferred income taxes\n279,941\n217,660\nOther liabilities\n125,188\n97,404\nTotal liabilities\n6,075,687\n5,272,411\nCommitments and contingencies\nStockholders equity:\n \n \nPreferred stock, $0.0001 par value; 2,000 shares authorized; none issued\n\n\nCommon stock, $0.0001 par value; 900,000 shares authorized; 600,834 shares issued; \n 491,262 and 494,254 shares outstanding, respectively\n61\n61\nAdditional paid-in-capital\n5,082,195\n4,616,331\nRetained earnings\n9,573,870\n8,114,517\nAccumulated other comprehensive income (loss)\n(111,821)\n(173,602)\nTreasury stock, at cost (109,572 and 106,580 shares, respectively), net of reissuances\n(6,084,436)\n(5,132,472)\nTotal stockholders equity\n8,459,869\n7,424,835\nTotal liabilities and stockholders equity\n$\n14,535,556\n$\n12,697,246\nSee accompanying Notes to Consolidated Financial Statements.','\n',char(10)),'Adobe''s FY2017 operating cash flow ratio is approximately 0.83. This is calculated by dividing cash from operations of $2,913,853 thousand by total current liabilities of $3,527,457 thousand (2,913,853 / 3,527,457 ≈ 0.83).',1,5376,797,0,11.66200000000000081,'financebench');
INSERT INTO runs VALUES('ca93164b-4ad5-4603-a42f-5afc352ec6bc','2026-08-26T05:30:04.705120+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium','claude-opus-4-8',3,replace('Question: Does Adobe have an improving operating margin profile as of FY2022? If operating margin is not a useful metric for a company like this, then state that and explain why.\n\nContext from financial filing:\nADOBE INC.\nCONSOLIDATED STATEMENTS OF INCOME\n(In millions, except per share data)\n \nYears Ended\n \nDecember 2,\n2022\nDecember 3,\n2021\nNovember 27,\n2020\nRevenue:\n \nSubscription\n$ \n16,388 $ \n14,573 $ \n11,626 \nProduct\n \n532 \n555 \n507 \nServices and other\n \n686 \n657 \n735 \nTotal revenue\n \n17,606 \n15,785 \n12,868 \n \nCost of revenue:\nSubscription\n \n1,646 \n1,374 \n1,108 \nProduct\n \n35 \n41 \n36 \nServices and other\n \n484 \n450 \n578 \nTotal cost of revenue\n \n2,165 \n1,865 \n1,722 \n \nGross profit\n \n15,441 \n13,920 \n11,146 \n \nOperating expenses:\nResearch and development\n \n2,987 \n2,540 \n2,188 \nSales and marketing\n \n4,968 \n4,321 \n3,591 \nGeneral and administrative\n \n1,219 \n1,085 \n968 \nAmortization of intangibles\n \n169 \n172 \n162 \nTotal operating expenses\n \n9,343 \n8,118 \n6,909 \n \nOperating income\n \n6,098 \n5,802 \n4,237','\n',char(10)),'No, Adobe''s operating margin is not improving as of FY2022. It declined to 34.6% from 36.8% in FY2021, though above FY2020''s 32.9%. The FY2022 drop reflects operating expenses rising to $9,343M from $8,118M (higher S&M and R&D) outpacing revenue growth. Operating margin is a useful metric for Adobe''s high-margin subscription software model.',1,5640,1059,1,15.31099999999999995,'financebench');
INSERT INTO runs VALUES('ac767c58-cb29-47a3-ae0d-144e4d94de6d','2026-08-26T05:30:17.034868+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium','claude-opus-4-8',3,replace('Question: Does Adobe have an improving Free cashflow conversion as of FY2022?\n\nContext from financial filing:\nADOBE INC.\n CONSOLIDATED STATEMENTS OF CASH FLOWS\n(In millions)\n \nYears Ended\n \nDecember 2,\n2022\nDecember 3,\n2021\nNovember 27,\n2020\nCash flows from operating activities:\n \n \nNet income\n$ \n4,756 \n$ \n4,822 \n$ \n5,260 \nAdjustments to reconcile net income to net cash provided by operating activities:\nDepreciation, amortization and accretion\n \n856 \n \n788 \n \n757 \nStock-based compensation\n \n1,440 \n \n1,069 \n \n909 \nReduction of operating lease right-of-use assets\n \n83 \n \n73 \n \n87 \nDeferred income taxes\n \n328 \n \n183 \n \n(1,501) \nUnrealized losses (gains) on investments, net\n \n29 \n \n(4) \n(11) \nOther non-cash items\n \n10 \n \n7 \n \n40 \nChanges in operating assets and liabilities, net of acquired assets and \n assumed liabilities:\nTrade receivables, net\n \n(198) \n(430) \n106 \nPrepaid expenses and other assets\n \n(94) \n(475) \n(288) \nTrade payables\n \n66 \n \n(20) \n96 \nAccrued expenses and other liabilities\n \n7 \n \n162 \n \n86 \nIncome taxes payable\n \n19 \n \n2 \n \n(72) \nDeferred revenue\n \n536 \n \n1,053 \n \n258 \nNet cash provided by operating activities\n \n7,838 \n \n7,230 \n \n5,727 \nCash flows from investing activities:\n \n \nPurchases of short-term investments\n \n(909) \n(1,533) \n(1,071) \nMaturities of short-term investments\n \n683 \n \n877 \n \n915 \nProceeds from sales of short-term investments\n \n270 \n \n191 \n \n167 \nAcquisitions, net of cash acquired\n \n(126) \n(2,682) \n \nPurchases of property and equipment\n \n(442) \n(348) \n(419) \nPurchases of long-term investments, intangibles and other assets\n \n(46) \n(42) \n(15) \nProceeds from sales of long-term investments and other assets\n \n \n \n \n \n9 \nNet cash used for investing activities\n \n(570) \n(3,537) \n(414)','\n',char(10)),'Yes, Adobe''s free cash flow conversion improved in FY2022. FCF (operating cash flow minus capex) was $7,396M ($7,838M - $442M) against net income of $4,756M, yielding a conversion of ~156%, up from ~143% in FY2021 and ~101% in FY2020. The rising conversion reflects strong operating cash flow growth relative to net income.',1,5002,881,0,12.32199999999999918,'financebench');
INSERT INTO runs VALUES('695bb1ca-3c4d-4009-853e-6a43b70455d2','2026-08-26T05:30:27.991136+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium','claude-opus-4-8',3,replace('Question: What is the quantity of restructuring costs directly outlined in AES Corporation''s income statements for FY2022? If restructuring costs are not explicitly outlined then state 0.\n\nContext from financial filing:\nConsolidated Statements of Operations\nYears ended December 31, 2022, 2021, and 2020\n2022\n2021\n2020\n(in millions, except per share amounts)\nRevenue:\nRegulated\n$\n3,538 \n$\n2,868 \n$\n2,661 \nNon-Regulated\n9,079 \n8,273 \n6,999 \nTotal revenue\n12,617 \n11,141 \n9,660 \nCost of Sales:\nRegulated\n(3,162)\n(2,448)\n(2,235)\nNon-Regulated\n(6,907)\n(5,982)\n(4,732)\nTotal cost of sales\n(10,069)\n(8,430)\n(6,967)\nOperating margin\n2,548 \n2,711 \n2,693 \nGeneral and administrative expenses\n(207)\n(166)\n(165)\nInterest expense\n(1,117)\n(911)\n(1,038)\nInterest income\n389 \n298 \n268 \nLoss on extinguishment of debt\n(15)\n(78)\n(186)\nOther expense\n(68)\n(60)\n(53)\nOther income\n102 \n410 \n75 \nLoss on disposal and sale of business interests\n(9)\n(1,683)\n(95)\nGoodwill impairment expense\n(777)\n \n \nAsset impairment expense\n(763)\n(1,575)\n(864)\nForeign currency transaction gains (losses)\n(77)\n(10)\n55 \nOther non-operating expense\n(175)\n \n(202)\nINCOME (LOSS) FROM CONTINUING OPERATIONS BEFORE TAXES AND EQUITY IN EARNINGS OF AFFILIATES\n(169)\n(1,064)\n488 \nIncome tax benefit (expense)\n(265)\n133 \n(216)\nNet equity in losses of affiliates\n(71)\n(24)\n(123)\nINCOME (LOSS) FROM CONTINUING OPERATIONS\n(505)\n(955)\n149 \nGain from disposal of discontinued businesses, net of income tax expense of $0, $1, and $0, respectively\n \n4 \n3 \nNET INCOME (LOSS)\n(505)\n(951)\n152 \nLess: Net loss (income) attributable to noncontrolling interests and redeemable stock of subsidiaries\n(41)\n542 \n(106)\nNET INCOME (LOSS) ATTRIBUTABLE TO THE AES CORPORATION\n$\n(546)\n$\n(409)\n$\n46','\n',char(10)),'AES Corporation''s FY2022 Consolidated Statements of Operations do not explicitly outline restructuring costs as a separate line item. Therefore, the quantity of restructuring costs directly outlined in the income statement is 0.',1,4883,608,0,10.94500000000000028,'financebench');
INSERT INTO runs VALUES('b51e0944-42ac-4e10-ad23-82aa27afddae','2026-08-26T05:30:44.476406+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium','claude-opus-4-8',3,replace('Question: Roughly how many times has AES Corporation sold its inventory in FY2022? Calculate inventory turnover ratio for the FY2022; if conventional inventory management is not meaningful for the company then state that and explain why.\n\nContext from financial filing:\nConsolidated Balance Sheets\nDecember 31, 2022 and 2021\n2022\n2021\n(in millions, except share and per share data)\nASSETS\nCURRENT ASSETS\nCash and cash equivalents\n$\n1,374 \n$\n943 \nRestricted cash\n536 \n304 \nShort-term investments\n730 \n232 \nAccounts receivable, net of allowance for doubtful accounts of $5 and $5, respectively\n1,799 \n1,418 \nInventory\n1,055 \n604','\n',char(10)),'Conventional inventory turnover is not meaningful for AES. AES is a power utility whose inventory is mainly fuel (coal, gas) and spare parts, not resalable goods, so it doesn''t ''sell'' inventory traditionally. Average inventory would be ($1,055M + $604M)/2 = $829.5M, but no COGS is provided, so a precise ratio cannot be computed.',1,4890,1204,1,16.47500000000000143,'financebench');
INSERT INTO runs VALUES('7bbbcd52-333d-4ef2-87d7-da017d6dfd49','2026-08-26T05:30:56.520386+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium','claude-opus-4-8',3,replace('Question: Based on the information provided primarily in the statement of financial position and the statement of income, what is AES''s FY2022 return on assets (ROA)? ROA is defined as: FY2022 net income / (average total assets between FY2021 and FY2022). Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\nContext from financial filing:\n128 \nConsolidated Balance Sheets\nDecember 31, 2022 and 2021\n2022\n2021\n(in millions, except share and per share data)\nASSETS\nCURRENT ASSETS\nCash and cash equivalents\n$\n1,374 \n$\n943 \nRestricted cash\n536 \n304 \nShort-term investments\n730 \n232 \nAccounts receivable, net of allowance for doubtful accounts of $5 and $5, respectively\n1,799 \n1,418 \nInventory\n1,055 \n604 \nPrepaid expenses\n98 \n142 \nOther current assets, net of CECL allowance of $2 and $0, respectively\n1,533 \n897 \nCurrent held-for-sale assets\n518 \n816 \nTotal current assets\n7,643 \n5,356 \nNONCURRENT ASSETS\nProperty, Plant and Equipment:\nLand\n470 \n426 \nElectric generation, distribution assets and other\n26,599 \n25,552 \nAccumulated depreciation\n(8,651)\n(8,486)\nConstruction in progress\n4,621 \n2,414 \nProperty, plant and equipment, net\n23,039 \n19,906 \nOther Assets:\nInvestments in and advances to affiliates\n952 \n1,080 \nDebt service reserves and other deposits\n177 \n237 \nGoodwill\n362 \n1,177 \nOther intangible assets, net of accumulated amortization of $434 and $385, respectively\n1,841 \n1,450 \nDeferred income taxes\n319 \n409 \nLoan receivable, net of allowance of $26\n1,051 \n \nOther noncurrent assets, net of allowance of $51 and $23, respectively\n2,979 \n2,188 \nNoncurrent held-for-sale assets\n \n1,160 \nTotal other assets\n7,681 \n7,701 \nTOTAL ASSETS\n$\n38,363 \n$\n32,963 \nLIABILITIES AND EQUITY\nCURRENT LIABILITIES\nAccounts payable\n$\n1,730 \n$\n1,153 \nAccrued interest\n249 \n182 \nAccrued non-income taxes\n249 \n266 \nAccrued and other liabilities\n2,151 \n1,205 \nNon-recourse debt, including $416 and $302, respectively, related to variable interest entities\n1,758 \n1,367 \nCurrent held-for-sale liabilities\n354 \n559 \nTotal current liabilities\n6,491 \n4,732 \nNONCURRENT LIABILITIES\nRecourse debt\n3,894 \n3,729 \nNon-recourse debt, including $2,295 and $2,223, respectively, related to variable interest entities\n17,846 \n13,603 \nDeferred income taxes\n1,139 \n977 \nOther noncurrent liabilities\n3,168 \n3,358 \nNoncurrent held-for-sale liabilities\n \n740 \nTotal nonc [...evidence truncated]','\n',char(10)),'AES''s FY2022 ROA is approximately -1.42% (about -0.01). This is calculated as the FY2022 net loss attributable to AES of roughly -$505 million divided by average total assets of $35,663 million (($38,363M + $32,963M) / 2). The negative ROA reflects that AES reported a net loss in FY2022.',1,5497,739,0,12.03700000000000081,'financebench');
INSERT INTO runs VALUES('a59173e7-7808-4a8b-bf2e-b7732d721c6f','2026-08-26T05:31:08.713498+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium','claude-opus-4-8',3,replace('Question: What is Amazon''s FY2017 days payable outstanding (DPO)? DPO is defined as: 365 * (average accounts payable between FY2016 and FY2017) / (FY2017 COGS + change in inventory between FY2016 and FY2017). Round your answer to two decimal places. Address the question by using the line items and information shown within the balance sheet and the P&L statement.\n\nContext from financial filing:\nTable of Contents\nAMAZON.COM, INC.\nCONSOLIDATED STATEMENTS OF OPERATIONS\n(in millions, except per share data)\n \n \nYear Ended December 31,\n \n2015\n \n2016\n \n2017\nNet product sales\n$\n79,268 $\n94,665 $\n118,573\nNet service sales\n27,738 \n41,322 \n59,293\nTotal net sales\n107,006 \n135,987 \n177,866\nOperating expenses:\n \n \n \nCost of sales\n71,651 \n88,265 \n111,934\nFulfillment\n13,410 \n17,619 \n25,249\nMarketing\n5,254 \n7,233 \n10,069\nTechnology and content\n12,540 \n16,085 \n22,620\nGeneral and administrative\n1,747 \n2,432 \n3,674\nOther operating expense, net\n171 \n167 \n214\nTotal operating expenses\n104,773 \n131,801 \n173,760\nOperating income\n2,233 \n4,186 \n4,106\nInterest income\n50 \n100 \n202\nInterest expense\n(459) \n(484) \n(848)\nOther income (expense), net\n(256) \n90 \n346\nTotal non-operating income (expense)\n(665) \n(294) \n(300)\nIncome before income taxes\n1,568 \n3,892 \n3,806\nProvision for income taxes\n(950) \n(1,425) \n(769)\nEquity-method investment activity, net of tax\n(22) \n(96) \n(4)\nNet income\n$\n596 $\n2,371 $\n3,033\nBasic earnings per share\n$\n1.28 $\n5.01 $\n6.32\nDiluted earnings per share\n$\n1.25 $\n4.90 $\n6.15\nWeighted-average shares used in computation of earnings per share:\n \n \n \nBasic\n467 \n474 \n480\nDiluted\n477 \n484 \n493\nSee accompanying notes to consolidated financial statements.\n38','\n',char(10)),'Amazon''s FY2017 DPO is approximately 92.78 days. Average accounts payable = (34,616 + 25,309)/2 = 29,962.5. COGS + change in inventory = 111,934 + (16,047 − 11,461) = 111,934 + 4,586 = 116,520. DPO = 365 × 29,962.5 / 116,520 ≈ 93.86 days.',1,4734,871,0,12.18399999999999928,'financebench');
INSERT INTO runs VALUES('164b2f15-bd98-45f6-bf03-fd9f48b45134','2026-08-26T05:31:18.269594+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium','claude-opus-4-8',3,replace('Question: What is Amazon''s year-over-year change in revenue from FY2016 to FY2017 (in units of percents and round to one decimal place)? Calculate what was asked by utilizing the line items clearly shown in the statement of income.\n\nContext from financial filing:\nTable of Contents\nAMAZON.COM, INC.\nCONSOLIDATED STATEMENTS OF OPERATIONS\n(in millions, except per share data)\n \n \nYear Ended December 31,\n \n2015\n \n2016\n \n2017\nNet product sales\n$\n79,268 $\n94,665 $\n118,573\nNet service sales\n27,738 \n41,322 \n59,293\nTotal net sales\n107,006 \n135,987 \n177,866\nOperating expenses:\n \n \n \nCost of sales\n71,651 \n88,265 \n111,934\nFulfillment\n13,410 \n17,619 \n25,249\nMarketing\n5,254 \n7,233 \n10,069\nTechnology and content\n12,540 \n16,085 \n22,620\nGeneral and administrative\n1,747 \n2,432 \n3,674\nOther operating expense, net\n171 \n167 \n214\nTotal operating expenses\n104,773 \n131,801 \n173,760\nOperating income\n2,233 \n4,186 \n4,106\nInterest income\n50 \n100 \n202\nInterest expense\n(459) \n(484) \n(848)\nOther income (expense), net\n(256) \n90 \n346\nTotal non-operating income (expense)\n(665) \n(294) \n(300)\nIncome before income taxes\n1,568 \n3,892 \n3,806\nProvision for income taxes\n(950) \n(1,425) \n(769)\nEquity-method investment activity, net of tax\n(22) \n(96) \n(4)\nNet income\n$\n596 $\n2,371 $\n3,033\nBasic earnings per share\n$\n1.28 $\n5.01 $\n6.32\nDiluted earnings per share\n$\n1.25 $\n4.90 $\n6.15\nWeighted-average shares used in computation of earnings per share:\n \n \n \nBasic\n467 \n474 \n480\nDiluted\n477 \n484 \n493\nSee accompanying notes to consolidated financial statements.\n38','\n',char(10)),'Amazon''s total net sales grew from $135,987 million in FY2016 to $177,866 million in FY2017, an increase of $41,879 million. This represents a year-over-year revenue growth of (177,866 - 135,987) / 135,987 = 30.8%.',1,4478,706,0,9.54599999999999938,'financebench');
INSERT INTO runs VALUES('42000590-9731-4d35-a496-947cd617c1e8','2026-08-26T05:31:28.307360+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium','claude-opus-4-8',3,replace('Question: By drawing conclusions from the information stated only in the income statement, what is Amazon''s FY2019 net income attributable to shareholders (in USD millions)?\n\nContext from financial filing:\nTable of Contents\nAMAZON.COM, INC.\nCONSOLIDATED STATEMENTS OF OPERATIONS\n(in millions, except per share data)\n \n \nYear Ended December 31,\n \n2017\n \n2018\n \n2019\nNet product sales\n$\n118,573 $\n141,915 $\n160,408\nNet service sales\n59,293 \n90,972 \n120,114\nTotal net sales\n177,866 \n232,887 \n280,522\nOperating expenses:\n \n \n \nCost of sales\n111,934 \n139,156 \n165,536\nFulfillment\n25,249 \n34,027 \n40,232\nTechnology and content\n22,620 \n28,837 \n35,931\nMarketing\n10,069 \n13,814 \n18,878\nGeneral and administrative\n3,674 \n4,336 \n5,203\nOther operating expense (income), net\n214 \n296 \n201\nTotal operating expenses\n173,760 \n220,466 \n265,981\nOperating income\n4,106 \n12,421 \n14,541\nInterest income\n202 \n440 \n832\nInterest expense\n(848) \n(1,417) \n(1,600)\nOther income (expense), net\n346 \n(183) \n203\nTotal non-operating income (expense)\n(300) \n(1,160) \n(565)\nIncome before income taxes\n3,806 \n11,261 \n13,976\nProvision for income taxes\n(769) \n(1,197) \n(2,374)\nEquity-method investment activity, net of tax\n(4) \n9 \n(14)\nNet income\n$\n3,033 $\n10,073 $\n11,588\nBasic earnings per share\n$\n6.32 $\n20.68 $\n23.46\nDiluted earnings per share\n$\n6.15 $\n20.14 $\n23.01\nWeighted-average shares used in computation of earnings per share:\n \n \n \nBasic\n480 \n487 \n494\nDiluted\n493 \n500 \n504\nSee accompanying notes to consolidated financial statements.\n38','\n',char(10)),'Amazon''s FY2019 net income attributable to shareholders was $11,588 million. The Consolidated Statements of Operations reports net income of $11,588 million with no non-controlling (minority) interest deduction, so net income attributable to shareholders equals the reported net income.',1,4403,643,0,10.02800000000000046,'financebench');
INSERT INTO runs VALUES('e29ea6b5-e78c-455c-8dfc-ab84d2a27848','2026-08-26T05:31:38.190119+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium','claude-opus-4-8',3,replace('Question: What is Amcor''s year end FY2020 net AR (in USD millions)? Address the question by adopting the perspective of a financial analyst who can only use the details shown within the balance sheet.\n\nContext from financial filing:\nAmcor plc and Subsidiaries\nConsolidated Balance Sheet\n(in millions)\nAs of June 30,\n2020\n2019\nAssets\nCurrent assets:\nCash and cash equivalents\n$\n742.6 \n$\n601.6 \nTrade receivables, net\n1,615.9 \n1,864.3 \nInventories, net\n1,831.9 \n1,953.8 \nPrepaid expenses and other current assets\n344.3 \n374.3 \nAssets held for sale\n \n416.1 \nTotal current assets\n4,534.7 \n5,210.1 \nNon-current assets:\nInvestments in affiliated companies\n77.7 \n98.9 \nProperty, plant and equipment, net\n3,614.8 \n3,975.0 \nOperating lease assets\n525.3 \n \nDeferred tax assets\n135.4 \n190.9 \nOther intangible assets, net\n1,994.3 \n2,306.8 \nGoodwill\n5,339.3 \n5,156.0 \nEmployee benefit assets\n43.4 \n40.2 \nOther non-current assets\n177.2 \n187.1 \nTotal non-current assets\n11,907.4 \n11,954.9 \nTotal assets\n$\n16,442.1 \n$\n17,165.0 \nLiabilities\nCurrent liabilities:\nCurrent portion of long-term debt\n$\n11.1 \n$\n5.4 \nShort-term debt\n195.2 \n788.8 \nTrade payables\n2,170.8 \n2,303.4 \nAccrued employee costs\n476.5 \n378.4 \nOther current liabilities\n1,120.0 \n1,044.9 \nLiabilities held for sale\n \n20.9 \nTotal current liabilities\n3,973.6 \n4,541.8 \nNon-current liabilities:\nLong-term debt, less current portion\n6,028.4 \n5,309.0 \nOperating lease liabilities\n465.7 \n \nDeferred tax liabilities\n672.4 \n1,011.7 \nEmployee benefit obligations\n391.7 \n386.8 \nOther non-current liabilities\n223.2 \n241.0 \nTotal non-current liabilities\n7,781.4 \n6,948.5 \nTotal liabilities\n11,755.0 \n11,490.3 \nCommitments and contingencies (See Note 19)\nShareholders'' Equity\nAmcor plc shareholders equity:\nOrdinary shares ($0.01 par value):\nAuthorized (9,000.0 shares)\nIssued (1,568.5 and 1,625.9 shares, respectively)\n15.7 \n16.3 \nAdditional paid-in capital\n5,480.0 \n6,007.5 \nRetained earnings\n246.5 \n323.7 \nAccumulated other comprehensive income (loss)\n(1,049.3)\n(722.4)\nTreasury shares (6.7 and 1.4 shares, respectively)\n(67.0)\n(16.1)\nTotal Amcor plc shareholders'' equity\n4,625.9 \n5,609.0 \nNon-controlling interest\n61.2 \n65.7 \nTotal shareholders'' equity\n4,687.1 \n5,674.7 \nTotal liabilities and  [...evidence truncated]','\n',char(10)),'Amcor''s year-end FY2020 net accounts receivable, shown as ''Trade receivables, net'' on the Consolidated Balance Sheet as of June 30, 2020, was $1,615.9 million (down from $1,864.3 million in FY2019).',1,5625,658,0,9.87400000000000055,'financebench');
INSERT INTO runs VALUES('fbb1cba7-5731-4ce7-91bf-0b77dbeabadd','2026-08-26T05:32:00.153236+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium','claude-opus-4-8',3,replace('Question: What was the key agenda of the AMCOR''s 8k filing dated 1st July 2022?\n\nContext from financial filing:\nOn June 30, 2022, Amcor Finance (USA), Inc. (the Former Issuer) and Amcor Flexibles North America, Inc. (the Substitute Issuer),\neach a wholly-owned subsidiary of Amcor plc (the Company), entered into a (i) Second Supplemental Indenture (the Second Supplemental\nIndenture) with the Trustee (as defined below) with respect to the Indenture, dated as of April 28, 2016 (as amended and/or supplemented to\ndate, the 2016 Indenture and, together with the Second Supplemental Indenture, the 2016 Indenture), among the Former Issuer, the\nguarantors party thereto and Deutsche Bank Trust Company Americas, as trustee (the Trustee), governing the Former Issuers (a) 3.625%\nGuaranteed Senior Notes due 2026 (the 2026 Notes) and (b) 4.500% Guaranteed Senior Notes due 2028 (the 2028 Notes and, together with\nthe 2026 Notes, the Existing Notes) and (ii) First Supplemental Indenture (the First Supplemental Indenture and, together with the Second\nSupplemental Indenture, the Supplemental Indentures) with the Trustee with respect to the Indenture, dated as of June 13, 2019 (as amended\nand/or supplemented to date, the 2019 Indenture and, together with the First Supplemental Indenture, the 2019 Indenture and, together with\nthe 2016 Indenture, the Indentures), among the Former Issuer, the guarantors party thereto and the Trustee, governing the Former Issuers\n(a) 3.625% Guaranteed Senior Notes due 2026 (the New 2026 Notes) and (b) 4.500% Guaranteed Senior Notes due 2028 (the New 2028\nNotes and, together with the New 2026 Notes, the New Notes), in each case, relating to the substitution of the Substitute Issuer for the Former\nIssuer and the assumption by the Substitute Issuer of the covenants of the Former Issuer under the Indentures. As disclosed in the Companys\nCurrent Report on Form 8-K, filed with the Securities and Exchange Commission (the SEC) on June 17, 2019, the New Notes were issued in\nJune 2019 following the completion of the Former Issuers exchange offer to certain eligible holders of t [...evidence truncated]','\n',char(10)),'The 8-K''s key agenda was the substitution of Amcor Flexibles North America, Inc. for Amcor Finance (USA), Inc. as issuer of certain senior notes. Via a Second Supplemental Indenture to the 2016 Indenture and a First Supplemental Indenture to the 2019 Indenture (dated June 30, 2022), the Substitute Issuer assumed the covenants for the 3.625% Notes due 2026 and 4.500% Notes due 2028.',1,10260,1711,2,21.95200000000000174,'financebench');
INSERT INTO runs VALUES('87805674-2a1f-48ed-b86e-fc79de9b78ec','2026-08-26T05:32:18.220064+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium','claude-opus-4-8',3,replace('Question: Has AMCOR''s quick ratio improved or declined between FY2023 and FY2022? If the quick ratio is not something that a financial analyst would ask about a company like this, then state that and explain why.\n\nContext from financial filing:\nAmcor plc and Subsidiaries\nConsolidated Balance Sheets\n($ in millions, except share and per share data)\nAs of June 30,\n2023\n2022\nAssets\nCurrent assets:\nCash and cash equivalents\n$\n689 \n$\n775 \nTrade receivables, net of allowance for credit losses of $21 and $25, respectively\n1,875 \n1,935 \nInventories, net\nRaw materials and supplies\n992 \n1,114 \nWork in process and finished goods\n1,221 \n1,325 \nPrepaid expenses and other current assets\n531 \n512 \nAssets held for sale, net\n \n192 \nTotal current assets\n5,308 \n5,853 \nNon-current assets:\nProperty, plant, and equipment, net\n3,762 \n3,646 \nOperating lease assets\n533 \n560 \nDeferred tax assets\n134 \n130 \nOther intangible assets, net\n1,524 \n1,657 \nGoodwill\n5,366 \n5,285 \nEmployee benefit assets\n67 \n89 \nOther non-current assets\n309 \n206 \nTotal non-current assets\n11,695 \n11,573 \nTotal assets\n$\n17,003 \n$\n17,426 \nLiabilities\nCurrent liabilities:\nCurrent portion of long-term debt\n$\n13 \n$\n14 \nShort-term debt\n80 \n136 \nTrade payables\n2,690 \n3,073 \nAccrued employee costs\n396 \n471 \nOther current liabilities\n1,297 \n1,344 \nLiabilities held for sale\n \n65 \nTotal current liabilities\n4,476 \n5,103 \nNon-current liabilities:\nLong-term debt, less current portion\n6,653 \n6,340 \nOperating lease liabilities\n463 \n493 \nDeferred tax liabilities\n616 \n677 \nEmployee benefit obligations\n224 \n201 \nOther non-current liabilities\n481 \n471 \nTotal non-current liabilities\n8,437 \n8,182 \nTotal liabilities\n$\n12,913 \n$\n13,285 \nCommitments and contingencies (See Note 20)\nShareholders'' Equity\nAmcor plc shareholders equity:\nOrdinary shares ($0.01 par value):\nAuthorized (9,000 million shares)\nIssued (1,448 and 1,489 million shares, respectively)\n$\n14 \n$\n15 \nAdditional paid-in capital\n4,021 \n4,431 \nRetained earnings\n865 \n534 \nAccumulated other comprehensive loss\n(862)\n(880)\nTreasury shares (1 and 2 million shares, respectively)\n(12)\n(18)\nTotal Amcor plc shareholders'' equity\n4,026 \n4,082 \nNon-controlling interests\n64 \n59 \nTotal shareholders'' equity\n4,090 \n4,141 \nTotal liabilities  [...evidence truncated]','\n',char(10)),'AMCOR''s quick ratio improved slightly from ~0.53 in FY2022 to ~0.57 in FY2023. FY2022: ($775M cash + $1,935M receivables)/$5,103M ≈ 0.53; FY2023: ($689M + $1,875M)/$4,476M ≈ 0.57. The gain came mainly from a larger drop in current liabilities than in liquid assets. This is a standard, relevant liquidity metric for a packaging company like Amcor.',1,7969,1316,1,18.05199999999999961,'financebench');
INSERT INTO runs VALUES('c94d96e7-3ca0-4057-9022-0e111c1e07a2','2026-08-26T05:32:34.051121+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium','claude-opus-4-8',3,replace('Question: What are major acquisitions that AMCOR has done in FY2023, FY2022 and FY2021?\n\nContext from financial filing:\nOn August 1, 2022, the Company completed the acquisition of 100% equity interest in a Czech Republic company that operates a world-class\nflexible packaging manufacturing plant. The purchase consideration of $59 million included a deferred portion of $5 million that was paid in the\nfirst quarter of fiscal year 2024. The acquisition is part of the Company''s Flexibles reportable segment and resulted in the recognition of acquired\nidentifiable net assets of $36 million and goodwill of $23 million. Goodwill is not deductible for tax purposes. The fair values of the identifiable\nnet assets acquired and goodwill are based on the Company''s best estimate as of June 30, 2023.\n On March 17, 2023, the Company completed the acquisition of 100% equity interest in a medical device packaging manufacturing site in\nShanghai, China. The purchase consideration of $60 million is subject to customary post-closing adjustments. The consideration includes\ncontingent consideration of $20 million, to be earned and paid in cash over the three years following the acquisition date, subject to meeting\ncertain performance targets. The acquisition is part of the Company''s Flexibles reportable segment and resulted in the recognition of acquired\nidentifiable net assets of $21 million and goodwill of $39 million. Goodwill is not deductible for tax purposes. The fair values of the contingent\nconsideration, identifiable net assets acquired, and goodwill are based on the Company''s best estimate as of June 30, 2023, and are considered\npreliminary. The Company aims to complete the purchase price allocation as soon as practicable but no later than one year from the date of the\nacquisition.\n On May 31, 2023, the Company completed the acquisition of a New Zealand based leading manufacturer of state-of-the-art, automated protein\npackaging machines. The purchase consideration of $45 million is subject to customary post-closing adjustments. The consideration includes\ncontingent consideration of $13 million, to b [...evidence truncated]','\n',char(10)),'In FY2023, Amcor made three Flexibles-segment acquisitions: a Czech Republic flexible packaging plant ($59M, Aug 1, 2022), a Shanghai medical device packaging site ($60M incl. $20M contingent, Mar 17, 2023), and a New Zealand protein packaging machine maker ($45M incl. $13M contingent, May 31, 2023). The context details no specific FY2022 or FY2021 acquisitions.',1,6679,1245,1,15.82199999999999918,'financebench');
INSERT INTO runs VALUES('e8f6359b-bc7c-4a46-9eef-68c19ba15328','2026-08-26T05:32:42.036912+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium','claude-opus-4-8',3,replace('Question: What industry does AMCOR primarily operate in?\n\nContext from financial filing:\nToday, we are a global leader in developing and producing responsible\npackaging for food, beverage, pharmaceutical, medical, home and personal-care, and other products','\n',char(10)),'Amcor primarily operates in the packaging industry. It is a global leader in developing and producing responsible packaging for a range of markets, including food, beverage, pharmaceutical, medical, and home and personal-care products.',1,2427,461,0,7.975999999999999979,'financebench');
INSERT INTO runs VALUES('7bed52a5-ec7f-4b8f-b5f1-3f9aebbb2ccb','2026-08-26T05:32:55.144064+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium','claude-opus-4-8',3,replace('Question: Does AMCOR have an improving gross margin profile as of FY2023? If gross margin is not a useful metric for a company like this, then state that and explain why.\n\nContext from financial filing:\nAmcor plc and Subsidiaries\nConsolidated Statements of Income\n($ in millions, except per share data)\nFor the years ended June 30,\n2023\n2022\n2021\nNet sales\n$\n14,694 \n$\n14,544 \n$\n12,861 \nCost of sales\n(11,969)\n(11,724)\n(10,129)\nGross profit\n2,725 \n2,820 \n2,732','\n',char(10)),'No, Amcor does not have an improving gross margin profile as of FY2023. Its gross margin declined to ~18.5% in FY2023 (2,725/14,694) from ~19.4% in FY2022 and ~21.2% in FY2021. Despite net sales rising to $14,694M, gross profit fell to $2,725M because cost of sales ($11,969M) grew faster than sales, compressing margins over the three-year period.',1,3081,946,0,13.09900000000000019,'financebench');
INSERT INTO runs VALUES('f584687d-8a15-4bc0-b2ac-58bb79ad7eb6','2026-08-26T05:33:11.119082+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium','claude-opus-4-8',3,replace('Question: What is the nature & purpose of AMCOR''s restructuring liability as oF Q2 of FY2023 close?\n\nContext from financial filing:\n($ in millions)\nEmployee Costs\nFixed Asset\nRelated Costs\nOther Costs\nTotal\nRestructuring\nCosts\nLiability balance at June 30, 2022\n$\n97 \n$\n3 \n$\n18 \n$\n118 \nNet charges to earnings\n2 \n \n \n2 \nCash paid\n(16)\n(1)\n(8)\n(25)\nReversal of unused amounts\n(2)\n \n \n(2)\nLiability balance at December 31, 2022\n$\n81 \n$\n2 \n$\n10 \n$\n93','\n',char(10)),'As of December 31, 2022 (Q2 FY2023 close), Amcor''s restructuring liability was $93 million, down from $118 million at June 30, 2022. It primarily covers workforce reductions/severance, as Employee Costs ($81M) dominate, plus Fixed Asset Costs ($2M) and Other Costs ($10M). It was reduced by $25M cash paid and $2M reversals, offset by $2M net charges.',1,4649,1121,1,15.96700000000000052,'financebench');
INSERT INTO runs VALUES('eab1ef5f-a45e-4fb8-8dbd-6195dd96ed6a','2026-08-26T05:33:24.863880+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium','claude-opus-4-8',3,replace('Question: What Was AMCOR''s Adjusted Non GAAP EBITDA for FY 2023\n\nContext from financial filing:\nTwelve Months Ended June 30, 2022\nTwelve Months Ended June 30, 2023\n($ million)\nEBITDA\nEBIT\nNet \nIncome\nEPS \n(Diluted\nUS \ncents)(1)\nEBITDA\nEBIT\nNet \nIncome\nEPS \n(Diluted \nUS \ncents)(1)\nNet income attributable to Amcor\n \n805 \n \n805 \n \n805 \n \n52.9 \n \n1,048 \n \n1,048 \n \n1,048 \n \n70.5 \nNet income attributable to non-controlling \ninterests\n \n10 \n \n10 \n \n10 \n \n10 \nTax expense\n \n300 \n \n300 \n \n193 \n \n193 \nInterest expense, net\n \n135 \n \n135 \n \n259 \n \n259 \nDepreciation and amortization\n \n579 \n \n569 \nEBITDA, EBIT, Net income and EPS\n \n1,829 \n \n1,250 \n \n805 \n \n52.9 \n \n2,080 \n \n1,510 \n \n1,048 \n \n70.5 \n2019 Bemis Integration Plan\n \n37 \n \n37 \n \n37 \n \n2.5 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nNet loss on disposals(2)\n \n10 \n \n10 \n \n10 \n \n0.7 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nImpact of hyperinflation\n \n16 \n \n16 \n \n16 \n \n1.0 \n \n24 \n \n24 \n \n24 \n \n1.9 \nProperty and other losses, net(3)\n \n13 \n \n13 \n \n13 \n \n0.8 \n \n2 \n \n2 \n \n2 \n \n0.1 \nRussia-Ukraine conflict impacts(4)\n \n200 \n \n200 \n \n200 \n \n13.2 \n \n(90) \n(90) \n(90) \n(6.0) \nPension settlements\n \n8 \n \n8 \n \n8 \n \n0.5 \n \n5 \n \n5 \n \n5 \n \n0.3 \nOther\n \n4 \n \n4 \n \n4 \n \n0.3 \n \n(3) \n(3) \n(3) \n(0.3) \nAmortization of acquired intangibles (5)\n \n163 \n \n163 \n \n10.7 \n \n160 \n \n160 \n \n10.8 \nTax effect of above items \n \n(32) \n(2.1) \n \n(57) \n(4.0) \nAdjusted EBITDA, EBIT, Net income and EPS \n \n2,117 \n \n1,701 \n \n1,224 \n \n80.5 \n \n2,018 \n \n1,608 \n \n1,089 \n \n73.3','\n',char(10)),'Amcor''s Adjusted (Non-GAAP) EBITDA for FY 2023 (twelve months ended June 30, 2023) was $2,018 million, down from $2,117 million in FY 2022. This was derived by adjusting the reported GAAP EBITDA of $2,080 million for items such as hyperinflation impact ($24M), Russia-Ukraine conflict impacts (-$90M), and others.',1,5336,901,0,13.73799999999999955,'financebench');
INSERT INTO runs VALUES('f6c57740-86f3-436d-8799-990e6e3e27a5','2026-08-26T05:33:38.050191+00:00','strict_repair','sequential_3node','medium','claude-opus-4-8',3,replace('Question: How much was the Real change in Sales for AMCOR in FY 2023 vs FY 2022, if we exclude the impact of FX movement, passthrough costs and one-off items?\n\nContext from financial filing:\nThree Months Ended June 30\nTwelve Months Ended June 30\n($ million)\nFlexibles\nRigid \nPackaging\nTotal\nFlexibles\nRigid \nPackaging\nTotal\nNet sales fiscal year 2023\n \n2,777 \n897 \n3,673 \n \n11,154 \n3,540 \n14,694 \nNet sales fiscal year 2022\n \n2,967 \n942 \n3,909 \n \n11,151 \n3,393 \n14,544 \nReported Growth %\n (6) \n (5) \n (6) \n \n 4 \n 1 \nFX %\n 1 \n (1) \n \n (4) \n (1) \n (3) \nConstant Currency Growth %\n (7) \n (4) \n (6) \n 4 \n 5 \n 4 \nRaw Material Pass Through %\n 1 \n \n 1 \n 5 \n 8 \n 5 \nItems affecting comparability %\n (3) \n \n (2) \n (2) \n \n (1) \nComparable Constant Currency \nGrowth %\n (5) \n (4) \n (5) \n 1 \n (3) \n \nVolume %\n (7) \n (6) \n (7) \n (3) \n (4) \n (3) \nPrice/Mix %\n 2 \n 2 \n 2 \n 4 \n 1 \n 3','\n',char(10)),'Excluding the impact of FX movement, raw material passthrough costs, and one-off items (items affecting comparability), Amcor''s real change in sales — measured as Comparable Constant Currency Growth — was 0% for FY2023 vs FY2022 at the total company level. This 0% comprised Flexibles at +1% and Rigid Packaging at -3%, and was made up of Volume -3% offset by Price/Mix +3%.',1,4129,979,0,13.18099999999999917,'financebench');
